{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g 'SKILL.md' -g '*RUN-LOG*' -g '*RUN-PLAN-v12*' -g '*OPERATING-CONTRACT*' -g '*DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16*' -g '\"'!poc/data/**'\"' -g '\"'!**/.mcp.json'\"' ; wc -l README.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md docs/poc/{pipeline-plan,service}.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g 'SKILL.md' -g '*RUN-LOG*' -g '*RUN-PLAN-v12*' -g '*OPERATING-CONTRACT*' -g '*DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16*' -g '\"'!poc/data/**'\"' -g '\"'!**/.mcp.json'\"' ; wc -l README.md docs/CURRENT_STATE.md docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md docs/poc/{pipeline-plan,service}.md\"","aggregated_output":"/Users/lchingen/hivemind\nclean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md\nAGENTS.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md\ndocs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md\n     208 README.md\n     865 docs/CURRENT_STATE.md\n     201 docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\n      67 docs/poc/pipeline-plan.md\n      85 docs/poc/service.md\n    1426 total\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; nl -ba docs/prd/README.md; nl -ba docs/poc/pipeline-plan.md; nl -ba docs/poc/service.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md; nl -ba docs/prd/README.md; nl -ba docs/poc/pipeline-plan.md; nl -ba docs/poc/service.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n     2\t\n     3\t> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n     4\t> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n     5\t> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n     6\t> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n     7\t> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n     8\t> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n     9\t> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n    10\t> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n    11\t\n    12\t## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n    13\t\n    14\tBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n    15\t\n    16\t1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n    17\t2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n    18\t3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n    19\t   word for it is already given.\n    20\t4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n    21\t   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n    22\t5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n    23\t   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n    24\t\n    25\t## 1. Goal (end to end)\n    26\t\n    27\tEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\n    28\tnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\n    29\tany one step's own score.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n    32\t\n    33\t**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n    34\t\n    35\tThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n    36\t\n    37\t- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n    38\t  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n    39\t  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n    40\t  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n    41\t  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n    42\t- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n    43\t  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n    44\t  nothing at all and were never judged.\n    45\t\n    46\t**Evidence:**\n    47\t\n    48\t| Evidence | Content | Source |\n    49\t|---|---|---|\n    50\t| Filing recognises only \"its own figure\" | The model says \"Broadcom's AI chip revenue is not cloud providers' spending on custom chips\" and \"Google Cloud's backlog is not capital spending\"; on a neutral refiling (no question id named, the rule not shown), 23 of 42 facts that matter are still not filed | `probe-filing-account-d1.md`, `refile-no_fallback.json` |\n    51\t| The judgement reasons onward once it has the facts | The custom-chip question's request of 7-14, sent 3 times in each of the three draws: as it was, 9/9 \"below $40 billion\"; with the Broadcom, Trainium and other facts written in that draw's ledger added, 9/9 left the lowest rung (6 times $40–60 billion, 3 times $60–80 billion), reasoning as the two readers did (\"guidance $16 billion, about $64 billion annualised, less networking chips\") | `predictions-one-test.md`, `one-test-result.json`, `log.md` row 8 |\n    52\t| Where the parking lot came from | D273: with the fallback sentence added, the core items on the extraction ruler went 64 → 68; the extraction ruler does not look at which question a fact is filed under (AGENTS §7.1, \"What it does not measure\") | `docs/DECISIONS.md` D273 |\n    53\t| What the parking lot holds | The figures above; an independent reader on 12 random articles: of 25 misfilings as it was, 21 were parked in the revenue question; 8 and 5 with the fallback deleted | `sink_by_standard.py`, the independent reader's report (`log.md` row 8) |\n    54\t\n    55\tAll files above are in `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/` unless noted otherwise.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 3. Ruled out or secondary (outside this phase's scope, each recorded separately)\n    58\t\n    59\t| Matter | Conclusion | Source |\n    60\t|---|---|---|\n    61\t| The argument card | Not built; its code is deleted (Alex: \"好，删掉卡片代码\") | `docs/research/2026-11-04-argument-record/log.md` rows 4–11 |\n    62\t| The arrows of the question graph | Really wired backwards: a seed question's arrow means \"supports\" (D208), while the judging code reads it as \"whose conclusion to read\" (PRD 06). As a result themes are judged only after the total-revenue question, so the total-revenue question reads the themes' conclusions of the previous day, and in draw 3 a chain of questions went wrong in turn. But it does not explain the misfiling (filing comes before judging), and the gold itself judges data-center revenue as \"total revenue × 92%\". A topic of its own; settled by D368 (item 4: the arrow means \"supports\"), its fix to be built and measured | `core-problem.md`, `review-core-codex.md` |\n    63\t| Code removes the total-revenue question's own figures | True (\"keep only the narrowest question\" takes a seed arrow for \"a narrower sub-question\"), but each draw it costs the total-revenue question only 0–2 of the facts it needs | `log.md` row 8 |\n    64\t| A filing re-check added at the end | Recovers some, and misfiles about as many (independent reader: about one misfiled for each one filed right); not the solution | The independent reader's report |\n    65\t| One general sentence, \"the other party's figure in a deal counts too\" | The custom-chip question gains, but it pulls Morgan Stanley's price for a Rubin rack from the Rubin revenue question to the capital-spending question, and leaves some facts with no home | `answers.json` (the two arms definition and definition_no_fallback), `measure-corrected.txt` |\n    66\t| Tightening D259's \"leeway\" | Alex's own decision; this phase does not overturn it | D259 |\n    67\t| Opening questions (the Ohio guarantee was not opened); the two readers' disagreement on the China-share question | Another class of problem, not in this phase | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/diagnosis.md` §0 |\n    68\t| Model drift | With the same requests, today about half as many facts go into the total-revenue question as on 11-03; the cause is not proven. Compare only with a control re-sent the same day, never with the archive | `log.md` row 6 |\n    69\t\n    70\t## 4. Constraints (what must not move, and why)\n    71\t\n    72\t- **The frozen ruler** (AGENTS §7.1, D261, D262): the extraction standard, version 5 (the signed version 3 with D248's seven boundaries and D250's four\n    73\t  evidence boundaries and counting unit), golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold; not a word changes; a disagreement is a gap in the\n    74\t  system (D263).\n    75\t- **The frozen extraction prompt** (D274): v74i (`poc-v108`) does not change by a word; what this phase changes is the one place that holds the filing\n    76\t  rule (the description of the `links` field), made into a new version with a new configuration and compared with v74i. Alex has given his word for this\n    77\t  work (2026-11-04: \"好你去解决问题\", \"开始做\"); making it the default needs his word again.\n    78\t- **Frozen opening and judging** (D354): this phase does not touch them.\n    79\t- **The standard's text** (in translation): §6, \"file under the narrowest open question; when no question takes it, record no home and drop it, and read\n    80\t  each one at evaluation\"; §5.1, \"makes the answer to some question more or less likely\", with four paths, \"demand, supply, competition, pricing\"; §5.3,\n    81\t  \"another company's matter is not kept when one cannot say which question about the covered company it moves through a path such as demand, supply,\n    82\t  competition or pricing\".\n    83\t- **Decisions**: D207 (one home per fact, the narrowest question), D209 (no home means dropped, each one read), D259 (leeway: a figure question takes\n    84\t  \"the demand, supply, competition and pricing that make it more or less likely\"; the total-revenue question taking more is an accepted cost, \"bounded by\n    85\t  the narrowest-question rule and the driver questions\"), D265 (code keeps only the narrowest), D273 (the fallback sentence), D355 (the two failed tests\n    86\t  of the previous filing topic).\n    87\t- **Prompt principles** (AGENTS §6): carry only the standard and add no new rule; a general hedge-fund analyst: every rule and example reads the same\n    88\t  for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip company, names neither NVIDIA nor its products or figures, and is not taken from a test article; a\n    89\t  classification is written as a closed set with two or three examples per class, replacing the abstract wording; quality is not fixed by lengthening\n    90\t  the prompt: remove as much as is added, or write down the reason.\n    91\t- **Code decides only \"is it the same thing\", never \"does it matter\"** (AGENTS §2).\n    92\t\n    93\t## 5. What to do\n    94\t\n    95\tStatus: the links-rule version that carried items 1–3 (v74l) failed its one test (§8 step 5); D361 keeps the fallback sentence, so item 3 is not carried out. The plan below is kept as written.\n    96\t\n    97\t1. **Replace the abstract sentence \"makes the answer more or less likely through demand, supply, competition or pricing\" with the few classes in which an analyst recognises an indirect relation, written as a closed set, each class with two or three examples from other industries** (AGENTS §6: one\n    98\t   abstract sentence is a coin flip on every send; examples replace it). A first draft of the classes (checked against the standard line by line at\n    99\t   design time; it holds only meanings the standard already has):\n   100\t   - The question's own figure: this question's figures, guidance, estimates and revisions (estimates for another period or another population follow\n   101\t     the existing rule).\n   102\t   - The other side of the same money: a seller's revenue from the buyers the question counts; those buyers' orders and backlog with these sellers.\n   103\t   - Price and volume: a product's price or shipments, for the question on that product's revenue.\n   104\t   - Variants and parts: a product's variants, alternatives, key parts and schedule changes, for the question on that product's launch.\n   105\t   - Demand, supply and competition moves with a number: customers' orders and capital-spending plans, suppliers' capacity and prices, competitors'\n   106\t     sales and share, for the question they affect.\n   107\t2. **State how to file a fact when two specific questions both bear on it** (the problem the `definition_no_fallback` arm exposed: Morgan Stanley's rack\n   108\t   price was filed under the buyers' capital-spending question, and the seller's Rubin revenue question lost it). One home per fact (D207): file it under\n   109\t   the question its figure measures most directly (for example, a hotel's room rate goes under its room-revenue question, not under the\n   110\t   travellers'-spending question; a parts maker's revenue from carmakers goes under the carmakers' parts-spending question, not under a rival parts\n   111\t   maker's share question); when one sentence gives a separate figure for each of two questions, write two items, each filed under its own question\n   112\t   (code stores two items that quote the same sentence as one record linked to both questions); never also file it under a broader question about the\n   113\t   same company (the failure of D355's test 2: the model filed it under an NVIDIA revenue question as well). The original draft's \"when the other\n   114\t   party's question is open … file it there as well\" conflicted with D207 and was deleted on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement (D360).\n   115\t3. **Delete the fallback sentence** and return to the standard's §6: when no question takes a fact, it is not filed; code drops it as \"no home\" and\n   116\t   records it (D209), and evaluation reads each one.\n   117\t4. Change only the one place that holds the filing rule; report its length in characters before and after, and write down the reason for any growth\n   118\t   (this time's reason: examples replace one abstract sentence).\n   119\t\n   120\t## 6. How to measure (the ruler does not move; the method does not change again)\n   121\t\n   122\t- **Requests**: rebuild each stored request byte for byte with the production code, changing only the one place that holds the filing rule; the\n   123\t  rebuilt request's archive id must equal the original's, or it is not sent (`resend_filing.py`, AGENTS §5). Never re-send the archive's copy whose\n   124\t  keys were sorted alphabetically.\n   125\t- **Articles**: development set = the 54 articles of `selection.json` (already read many times); held-out set = articles the extraction gold read and\n   126\t  the judgement gold uses that are not among those 54, drawn once before the test, with the list frozen and never changed afterwards. Both sets are\n   127\t  measured and reported apart.\n   128\t- **Control**: the unchanged requests re-sent 3 times the same day. No comparison with the archive (model drift).\n   129\t- **Each answer**: goes through the production code checks and filing first (including \"keep only the narrowest\" and \"no home means dropped\"), and is\n   130\t  counted after.\n   131\t- **What is counted** (`measure_filing.py`, with its matching rule fixed: items are matched on the distinct numbers and the proper nouns other than\n   132\t  NVIDIA in the source text; a source passage the system quotes that overlaps the gold's passage also counts as the same matter; \"optional\" counts as\n   133\t  optional; the citations in the body of the gold's answers are read):\n   134\t  - whether each fact that matters (one the gold uses to judge any question) reached the question that uses it, counted per fact, not per \"fact ×\n   135\t    question\";\n   136\t  - per question: the facts that matter that reached it and the new noise that appeared, counted by the decision rule below;\n   137\t  - the parking lot: how many facts the total-revenue question (v1, together with v22) takes in, and what they are;\n   138\t  - noise: how many of the sentences the standard does not keep are kept; per question, how noise is filed (links on facts the standard does not keep\n   139\t    or no question uses);\n   140\t  - how many core and supporting facts appear; the facts that matter that are dropped; how many questions each fact is filed under on average.\n   141\t  - Matching still has a small error (spot check: the new rule mismatches a few and misses a few); say so when reporting the numbers, and read the\n   142\t    ones that matter one by one.\n   143\t- **Decision rule** (rewritten on 2026-11-04 with Alex's agreement, D360. The original draft required \"every draw above the highest of the control's\n   144\t  three\" and the like, which the control itself fails when measured again, so it cannot tell good from bad. Script `decide.py`; written down and frozen\n   145\t  before each test; each draw's numbers and the totals are all reported):\n   146\t  - **Totals, with luck ruled out first**: each article's 6 answers from the new and the old arm are randomly reassigned between the two arms 4,000\n   147\t    times (with a fixed random seed); the new rule's difference counts as a real change only when fewer than 5% of the reassignments reach it.\n   148\t    - Development set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must rise; the count under the total-revenue questions (v1, v22) must\n   149\t      fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core facts must not fall.\n   150\t    - Held-out set: facts that matter reaching the question that uses them must not fall; sentences the standard does not keep must not rise; core\n   151\t      facts must not fall.\n   152\t  - **Per question, counted by code against the gold** (development and held-out sets together):\n   153\t    - A fact that matters which reaches the question at least two fewer times under the new rule than under the control counts as \"one fewer\" for that\n   154\t      question; in under the control all three times and under the new rule none of the three, it counts as \"lost outright\". A move to another question\n   155\t      that the gold also uses it for and that is no broader than the original (one home per fact, D207) does not count as fewer; a move to a broader\n   156\t      question counts. \"One more\" in the opposite direction offsets it.\n   157\t    - A noise sentence (not kept by the standard, or kept but used by no question) that the new rule files under the question at least twice while the\n   158\t      control never does counts as \"new noise\"; filed all three times, it counts as \"outright new noise\". The opposite direction offsets it.\n   159\t    - Any question with a net 3 fewer, a net 2 lost outright, a net 5 more new noise, or a net 3 more outright new noise fails. These thresholds are the\n   160\t      smallest values that keep \"misjudged when new and old are exactly the same\" within about one in ten; at the test, first compute the\n   161\t      misjudgement rate from the reassignments; if it is above one in ten, raise the threshold most prone to misjudgement by one step, then compare with\n   162\t      what was measured.\n   163\t  - **Smaller changes** (a question 2 fewer, two or three more noise) cannot be told from luck in three measurements: all are listed, given to a reader\n   164\t    who did not write the rule to read against the raw answers, and written into the report; they do not decide pass or fail.\n   165\t  Only when everything passes does it become a candidate; a candidate runs end to end three times on the 14-day window (extraction re-sent, matching and\n   166\t  judging run as usual, scored on the frozen judgement ruler), and that step needs Alex's word.\n   167\t\n   168\t## 7. Process (no drift)\n   169\t\n   170\t- **One recommendation and one test at a time** (AGENTS §7.3, Alex: \"就测试一种，一次\"): write the design first and put it through rounds of review (own\n   171\t  checks, Codex read-only, a reader who did not write it reading the raw records) until a round finds nothing serious; write down and freeze the\n   172\t  predictions and stop conditions; test once; if it does not match, stop and take it to Alex (D355: \"如果有什么不符合hypothesis 的地方你需要终止然后和我说我们重新评估\").\n   173\t- **A review's report is a lead**: each finding counts only once it is checked against the records (AGENTS §1).\n   174\t- **A prompt audit before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`): check the whole request against the standard sentence by\n   175\t  sentence, in both directions.\n   176\t- **Every step goes into `log.md`**: what was done, what came of it, the files; mistakes and overturned results stay in it too.\n   177\t- **Do not repeat what was ruled out** (§3).\n   178\t\n   179\t## 8. Steps and where we are\n   180\t\n   181\t| # | Step | Output | Status |\n   182\t|---|---|---|---|\n   183\t| 1 | Design: the classes of indirect relation (a closed set + examples from other industries), how to file when a fact measures both sides, deleting the fallback; checked against the standard sentence by sentence; length before and after | `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/design-links.md` | Done (draft 3, v74l / poc-v115) |\n   184\t| 2 | Checks at no cost: a sample answer written by the new rule goes through every code check; the new rule's text goes through the prompt audit | Same as above | Done |\n   185\t| 3 | Review rounds (Codex, independent readers, own), until nothing serious remains | `review-links-*.md` | Done (Codex 7 rounds, independent readers 3 times; round 7 found nothing serious) |\n   186\t| 4 | Draw and freeze the held-out set; write down and freeze this test's predictions and decision rule | `heldout.json`, `predictions-links.md` | Done (sha256 recorded for both) |\n   187\t| 5 | Test once: development set + held-out set, the new rule and the same-day control 3 times each | `answers-links.json`, `log.md` | Done (2026-11-04): failed: facts that matter reaching the right question, development set +7 (p 0.26); every other condition passed |\n   188\t| 6 | If passed: to Alex, to decide whether to run three 14-day end-to-end draws; if failed: stop, to Alex | `design-links.md` §8 | Stopped, to Alex |\n   189\t| 7 | (D361, Alex: \"按照你的提以来，但是你一定是自己反思多轮之后才给出的行动\") Measure the ceiling first: hand the source passages the gold cites straight to the frozen judgement; the custom-chip, operating-margin and capital-spending questions judged 3 times each at the window end, the custom-chip question as it was 3 times as the control; the fallback sentence is not deleted for now | `reflection-v74l.md`, `oracle-design.md` | Done: operating margin grounded 3/3, capital spending 2/3, custom chips 0/3 (the gap is in interpretation); the next step waits for Alex |\n   190\t| 8 | (D362, Alex: \"你继续啊不要scope drift and strongly follow evidence of your discovery and learning\") Two small changes where facts are not delivered, one at a time: ① when a quote is rewritten, code matches it back to the source sentence (changing nothing the model sees; verified by replaying the archive); ② a figure question's description states which figures it is derived from by definition (first sent 3 times each on the article that motivated it) | `delivery-fixes.md` | Stopped: an independent check overturned the first change's code (it would take in wrong figures); the second failed its trial send (1/3). The root: the signed standard's §5.3 does not keep two key facts, treating them as \"a restatement of known facts\" (proposal 6, awaiting approval); to Alex |\n   191\t| 9 | **Historical; superseded by D370.** (D363, Alex: \"乙\") At coverage start, record each question's starting figure once from the company's latest earnings report; the daily path still takes no restatements: design, rounds of self-checks and independent checks, validated first on the archive in the simplest way | `baseline-design.md` | Validated on two questions: the frozen extraction does not read the report's tables (0/3); with each question reading the report once on its own, the operating-margin question read its starting figure 2/3 and the capital-spending question 3/3, and the judgement then judged right (2/3, 3/3), about 2.5 cents. All 22 questions tested once (2026-11-05, Alex: \"你充分验证\"; files chosen by code, a same-day control, about $0.43): v10 and v12 judged right 9/9 each; no question already judged right turned wrong; but 5 of the 53 figures read were other quantities (the Vera CPU question and the Blackwell and Rubin question took a larger or neighbouring revenue line), so it failed condition 3, written beforehand. The model itself pointed to the reading instructions' sentence \"write the lines it is computed from, or the line the report uses for it\"; stopped, to Alex. Alex: \"改\"; only this sentence was changed (\"write only the lines it is computed from, or the same quantity under another name; never a broader line or a line next to it\"), tried on 4 questions first, then tested on all 22 (about $0.36): all three conditions passed: v10 and v12 9/9 each, no question judged right turned wrong, and all 46 figures were the questions' own quantities. Checked after an independent reader's challenge: the new sentence made the reading miss the capital-spending line in 3 of 12 reads, and it had been tested only on the one May report; the fourth version (a line-by-line re-check after writing) removed too much and failed; after a census of every failure trace (Alex: \"我需要你深度看完所有失败trace给我准信\"), the third version with the repaired quote check was chosen (D365): 0 wrong figures on the held-out August report; built into the production system (poc-v116; the $0 checks and the tests all pass). The full acceptance, three 14-day draws (Alex: \"跑验收\"; a same-day control; about $4.79): all passed: operating margin and capital spending judged right 3/3 each (the control never judged them), 53 against 45 in total (of 57); for the China-share question, the only one two fewer, both readers found the starting figures did not lead it astray. Alex: \"都做\": poc-v116 became the default (D366); the code was committed and a PR opened |\n   192\t\n   193\t## 9. Alex's own words on this matter (in time order)\n   194\t\n   195\t- \"你这个单独开一个专题出来解决 … 是promot，是系统结构设计还是到底哪里有问题挂错一堆东西，机制是不是有问题（一定要挂吗？有没有可能是杂音\" (2026-11-03)\n   196\t- \"So if it's a customer problem or other companies that you think is relating NVIDIA and it's the wrong evidence, then what is the standard for it? … from a hedge fund analyst standpoint, are these even relevant? Do you think your ruler is wrong in this aspect?\" (2026-11-04)\n   197\t- \"你确定下你接下来要改进的量化目标，以及你要看到端到端我们的管线是要做什么的，不要乱硬优化，要看到目标\" (2026-11-04)\n   198\t- \"就测试一种，一次 … 能不能自己有个超级自信的定论和证据再给我提建议\" (2026-11-04)\n   199\t- \"这些只是类型题，你需要抽象拔高我们现在的核心问题和卡点。这个很重要这样改下去才是对的\" (2026-11-04)\n   200\t- \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\" (2026-11-04)\n   201\t- \"go\" (2026-11-04, on the proposed amendments to §5.2 and §6 and on this test)\n     1\t# PRD index\n     2\t\n     3\tEach module has one Markdown specification. Select a module on `docs/system-design.html`, then open its PRD link. The system PRD link at the top opens the overview.\n     4\t\n     5\t| File | Module | Status |\n     6\t|---|---|---|\n     7\t| 00-system.md | System overview | Main flow defined |\n     8\t| 01-universe.md | Research universe | First version frozen; specific themes remain a draft |\n     9\t| 02-classification.md | Upstream classification | First version frozen |\n    10\t| 03-config.md | Config | First version frozen |\n    11\t| 04-dispatch.md | Dispatch | First version frozen |\n    12\t| 05-extraction.md | Extraction (read-only) | Foundation frozen; v74i prompt remains in use; separate report import retired and v117 replay not yet accepted end to end (D370) |\n    13\t| 05-extraction-standard.html | Extraction standard: ledger records, question types and opening, evidence and weight, golden set, prompt measurement | Signed (D243), open it in a browser; the one measuring standard for extraction, replacing `docs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md` (D229) |\n    14\t| 05-extraction-standard-corrections.md | Dated corrections to the frozen standard page: the statements later decisions changed, read with the page and never merged into it | Three records, 2026-11-05 (D368) |\n    15\t| 06-views.md | Thesis library and matching records | Foundation frozen; group handoff and storage in the default pipeline (D165); other open items are in the PRD |\n    16\t| 07-final-verdict.md | Closing at the due day (the final verdict retired, D356) | Decided and built (D54, D356) |\n    17\t| 08-pull-mode.md | Pull mode | Decided and built (D54, D356) |\n    18\t| 09-review-pool.md | Human review pool | First version frozen; intake defined, output deferred |\n    19\t| 10-filing-phase.md | Current phase: filing that links facts the way an analyst does (in English since D368) | In progress since 2026-11-04; read before every step |\n    20\t| 11-production-experiments-from-split.md | Production experiments drawn from the failed split extraction: what to try on production, the evidence for each, and what not to repeat | Proposed 2026-11-06 (D378); each experiment opens only on Alex's word |\n    21\t\n    22\t## Maintenance rules\n    23\t\n    24\tAuthority: Alex's instructions, then `docs/DECISIONS.md`, then the PRDs and design page. If a PRD contradicts a decision, correct the PRD.\n    25\t\n    26\tAfter **every change**, reason through every module in this order: research universe → classification → Config → dispatch → extraction → thesis library → closing at the due day → pull mode → human review pool → system overview. Compare each with the decisions and ask:\n    27\t\n    28\t1. Does this change affect the module?\n    29\t2. Do its page and PRD describe the same behaviour?\n    30\t3. Do the examples still work?\n    31\t4. Do the acceptance criteria or open questions need to change?\n    32\t\n    33\tCorrect inconsistencies and list what changed and why. Update decisions and current state in the same slice.\n    34\t\n    35\tRun `python3 docs/check.py` **last**. It compares generated PRD blocks with page data, rebuilds the bundle used when the page opens from disk, runs the classification simulation and checks rendering. It reports mismatches rather than correcting their meaning. A pass is a mechanical result, not the reasoning review.\n     1\t# POC pipeline: the implementation of the design\n     2\t\n     3\tUpdated November 5, 2026. This is the working implementation overview: what each stage does in code and where it is specified. The PRDs own what each module must do, [decisions](../DECISIONS.md) own what is decided, and the [current state](../CURRENT_STATE.md) owns open defects and the next task. The original plan and the run history up to October 25 are preserved in the [historical snapshot](pipeline-plan-history-2026-10-25.zh.md); the original fifteen-task plan is `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-10-23-nvda-poc.md`.\n     4\t\n     5\t## What runs\n     6\t\n     7\tThe replay default is `poc/config/poc-v118.json` (D370, D374): the frozen v108 extraction, matching and judging settings without seeded questions, a separate coverage-start report reading or the unused sampling setting. Existing article-created questions remain context. An earnings release can still enter as a source item. This configuration is not yet accepted end to end.\n     8\t\n     9\t| Stage | What it does | Code | Specified in |\n    10\t|---|---|---|---|\n    11\t| Dispatch | Each item in order: three questions, a sequence number, a processing record; items before coverage start are not dispatched | `dispatch.py` | PRD 04 |\n    12\t| Extraction | One call per item reads the whole item with the questions open at the start of its day and their takes lines, and files each statement under the questions it bears on; a statement no question takes is dropped as no home (D209, D264); extraction v74i at low reasoning (D274) | `extraction.py`, `placement.py` (the takes lines and the links rule) | PRD 05 and the extraction standard |\n    13\t| Matching and writing | Jev asks each candidate's relation to the listed theses and each evidence piece's relation to its thesis's pieces (D169); the collision judge decides when a candidate collides with an open question (D190); themes are written first | `views/jev_matching.py`, `views/matching.py`, `jev.py`, `views/writing.py`, `views/groups.py` | PRD 06 |\n    14\t| Judging | Once at the end of each day, the open questions whose evidence changed, dependencies first (D63); judging-v7 reads the evidence in view (D198, D263) | `views/round.py`, `views/judging.py` | PRD 06 |\n    15\t| Pull mode | At the end of each day, every question whose due day has come closes at the rung it stands on; one never judged closes with \"no data to verify\"; nothing is re-judged (D356) | `pull.py` | PRD 08 |\n    16\t| Replay | Items advance by day with a future-input guard; a run cannot start before a completed day; the parallel day prepares extraction requests early and re-checks each at its turn (D181–D183) | `replay.py`, `parallel_day.py`, `scripts/replay.py` | D159, D183 |\n    17\t\n    18\t## Product path and POC simplifications\n    19\t\n    20\tThe old repository contributes requirements and two mechanisms: request-keyed model caching and archive, and append-only historical records. Its debates, loop and read model are not ported. The corpus is the team's source store; the pipeline retrieves originals by id rather than copying them into the thesis ledger.\n    21\t\n    22\t| Module | Limit or deliberate simplification |\n    23\t|---|---|\n    24\t| Research universe | `poc/universe.json` (NVDA, peers and themes) is a manually maintained POC list |\n    25\t| Classification | Corpus assembly supplies categories, subjects, copies and feed flags; the tables cover acquired channels, not the full upstream design |\n    26\t| Dispatch | Return-to-upstream is recorded, not notified |\n    27\t| Extraction | One company and one window measured; quotations are checked against the source, generated summaries and numbers are not (an exact quote does not prove an accurate summary) |\n    28\t| Thesis storage | Append-only SQLite tables with mutation-refusal triggers and full-text search; no human labels or trained smaller model yet |\n    29\t| Matching | The same figure in another document is still often judged new (build log row 49) |\n    30\t| Writing | Single process; no distributed queues |\n    31\t| Pull mode | No separate final verdict and no day-end re-judging (D356) |\n    32\t| Replay | Replay cannot measure live latency; model knowledge can extend beyond a replay window, so the input guard is not proof against model look-ahead |\n    33\t\n    34\t## Model and configuration\n    35\t\n    36\tExtraction and judging run on OpenAI `gpt-6-luna` (D62); matching runs on TypeSafe's Jev `jev-1.14.0` (D169). Keep provider and model explicit; never switch silently. Measurements report cost from the archives' tokens at the published rates in `hivemind.model.PRICES`, never from an archive's stored cost; the replay's spend cap and daily log read the archive's stored `cost_usd`. The Idea API runs `poc-v118-idea` (D290, D335, D374).\n    37\t\n    38\t## Run settings and timing\n    39\t\n    40\t`scripts/replay.py` prepares extraction requests 16 at a time (`--workers`; `--workers 1` is the serial path; the measurement launcher passes its `HIVEMIND_WORKERS` there), sends Jev matching 8 at a time (`--jev-workers`) and judgements 32 at a time (`judging.JUDGE_WORKERS`; a day's round judges one dependency level at a time, themes before assets; on the 2026-11-02 runs a round held up to 40 judgements in two levels). A model call times out after 180 s and is tried up to three times (`model.openai_transport`); a Jev call after 60 s, also up to three times (`jev.transport`). The slowest honest answers seen take about 40 s (a judgement) to 60 s (an extraction). A measurement runs its three draws at the same time, one launcher each (`docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/harness/run_arm.sh`); no rate-limit error (HTTP 429) has been seen, at peaks of about 170 concurrent requests over three draws.\n    41\t\n    42\tMeasured on 2026-11-02 (arm 4, before the one-decider architecture: three draws of 14 days, 243 items each): 75 minutes of wall clock for all three against 3.3 hours of model time a draw (extraction 243 calls, 15 s on average; placement 212 calls, 9.4 s; Jev matching 461 calls, 1.6 s; judgements 356 calls, 16 s). About 45 minutes a draw was the serial chain at each item's turn (placement about 9 s, matching and the write about 3 s), because the separate placement call read the questions open at that moment; a day's judging round took about 16 minutes at 6 workers and about 2 at 32. The one-decider architecture (D264) removed the placement call from the chain: the extraction reads the day-start list and files the statements itself. The 14-day, three-draw acceptance of 2026-11-05 cost about $0.8 a draw at published rates.\n    43\t\n    44\t## Verification and run discipline\n    45\t\n    46\tThe working rules own the procedure (AGENTS §5 and §7): replay stored answers through the current checks with `scripts/preflight.py` before any paid run and read every changed refusal ; prove the decisive case on stored data; a cached replay establishes code behaviour, not model consistency; a comparison of totals uses three draws beside a fresh control; no prompt or package code changes while a run is live.\n    47\t\n    48\tThe original product acceptance also includes:\n    49\t\n    50\t- Valid judgement citations to evidence in force and dependency theses; first judgements and score changes declare a changed conclusion.\n    51\t- Cached replay without model calls and with the same result.\n    52\t- No reads of future items during replay.\n    53\t- A labelled table for twenty user ideas, source-backed trigger records and false all-clear days.\n    54\t\n    55\tMissing gold or unexercised paths are reported, not counted as passed.\n    56\t\n    57\t## Service and frontend\n    58\t\n    59\tThe read-only service provides the complete dated library, search, historical thesis and evidence reads and numeric claim evaluation. The UI opens that library and offers a separate Idea flow with weighted theses and historical confidence; its Trigger and Action controls are frontend previews. See [service](service.md), [Idea card](idea-card.md) and [Thesis UI](thesis-ui.md) for their contracts, and the [README](../../README.md#run-the-service-for-testing) for host startup and local-data requirements.\n    60\t\n    61\t## Known limits\n    62\t\n    63\tPrompt quality can produce missing, overly broad or trivial questions. Code keys do not prove semantic identity or correct grouping; one matter can still end up in several research groups. Corpus classification covers only observed channels. Some original-source locations and input supersession paths remain simplified. Event-dependency succession and large-library recall need path-specific validation. Consult the current state before launching further work.\n    64\t\n    65\t## Historical records\n    66\t\n    67\tThe [snapshot](pipeline-plan-history-2026-10-25.zh.md) preserves the initial build sequence, the early replays, model comparisons, the preflight diagnosis, the daily-judgement change, sealed-run results, corpus-tag corrections and early UI iterations, in their original language. The configurations, prompt versions and mechanisms retired since (the separate placement call, extraction versions before v74i, judging versions before v7) are recorded in the [decision log](../DECISIONS-log.md); current instructions above supersede historical proposals to rerun or tune.\n     1\t# Hivemind service: a thesis database with search\n     2\t\n     3\tD76, October 24, 2026; English edition and current-document refresh, October 25 (D163). Alex defined Hivemind as a database with a search engine on top. Its read service lets an agent or page find theses, choose what to use and inspect the state, history and evidence. Hivemind stores shared **theses**, not user ideas, positions or commitments. This distinction replaces the ambiguous use of “thesis” for both in the earlier document. The [historical source](../research/2026-10-25-english/service.original.md) retains prior wording and verification details.\n     4\t\n     5\tThe core read service does not choose or arrange results for a caller. The separate [`POST /ideas` application flow](idea-card.md) discovers candidate theses, then adopts and weights them in a final assessment. The default `poc-v118-idea` configuration uses two discovery calls and one final assessment; older configurations retain their own contracts. The Idea specification owns those steps and their response. The route returns a UI-ready response.\n     6\t\n     7\t## Historical visibility\n     8\t\n     9\tReaders accept an `as_of` calendar day, defaulting to the ledger's last completed day. They do not use today's clock to reinterpret history. A row is visible when its dispatch sequence belongs to an item or daily round dated on or before that day. This applies to theses, switches, judgements, evidence links, sources, replacements, dependencies and resolutions. `history` and `changes` use explicit date spans.\n    10\t\n    11\tThe historical sealed-ledger check found 28 dependency rows and 750 source rows newer than the theses/judgements they attach to; those rows must stay hidden in earlier reads. A later quotation or relationship must not appear in an old snapshot.\n    12\t\n    13\t## Interfaces and readers\n    14\t\n    15\tField and route names are compatibility identifiers; `view` in an API name means the glossary's **thesis**.\n    16\t\n    17\t| Interface | Returned content | Reader |\n    18\t|---|---|---|\n    19\t| `search(query, k, as_of, include_closed)` | Query/day, semantic or lexical mode, missing current vectors, ranked results or null; each result includes ID, subject, kind, focus, question, due date, lifecycle, relationships and score components | Caller chooses relevant theses; search evaluation |\n    20\t| `views(object, as_of, include_closed)` | Thesis records | Agent or page |\n    21\t| `view(view_id, as_of)` | Identity, source, dates, ladder, current state or null, dependencies, dependants and counts | Agent or detail panel |\n    22\t| `history(view_id, from_day, to_day)` | Judgements, rung changes, evidence arrivals, replacements and lifecycle switches | History chart and replay |\n    23\t| `evidence(view_id, as_of, tier)` | Evidence in force, original quotes, summaries, tiers, speakers, dates and sources | Agent or evidence list |\n    24\t| `record(view_id, as_of)` | Last completed check with day/round and processed/arriving counts, or null | “Last checked” display |\n    25\t| `changes(since_day, to_day)` | Changed judgements with previous/current positions, reasons and historically visible citations | Polling agent |\n    26\t| `evaluate(view_id, comparator, value_usd, as_of)` | Whether evaluable and why; otherwise claim position, judged position/label/condition and verdict | Numeric claim display |\n    27\t| `thesis_bundles(view_ids, as_of)` / `GET /thesis-bundles?ids=...` | Complete dated bundles with ladder, judgements, historical evidence, sources, dependencies and switches | `fromBundles` in the modular UI |\n    28\t| `thesis_library(as_of)` / `GET /library?as_of=...` | Complete dated library and research groups under the [library envelope](#current-library-envelope-d275) | Library UI and agents |\n    29\t| `POST /ideas` | Idea, search summary, mapped parts and chosen theses, related theses, evaluation, optional bundles, days, trace and cost | Idea application/UI; see its contract |\n    30\t| Derived index | Content hashes, embedding model, vectors, token counts and build timestamps | Search only; not the thesis ledger |\n    31\t\n    32\tThe service returns stored prose as stored. D275 points the read-service defaults at the collected English Jev library and its matching items database, with a separate derived-index directory. Explicit old-ledger paths remain supported. This changes neither matching semantics nor the configured Idea prompt.\n    33\t\n    34\t## Numeric claim evaluation\n    35\t\n    36\tUse structured numeric rung bounds where supplied; the legacy path parses text. Bounds must form an unbroken, non-overlapping partition. Each shared boundary belongs to exactly one band; a lowest money range may begin at zero. A non-money or unparseable ladder returns `evaluable: false` with a reason.\n    37\t\n    38\tLegacy parsing recognises comparator phrases, ranges, postfix bounds and exact amounts. It follows the clauses specifying the amount, ignoring preceding datelines and later remarks. A bare nonzero number is not automatically money; percentages, gigawatts and years are not dollar amounts. Chinese magnitude units remain accepted input. For example, 269.79 hundred-million dollars is exactly $26,979,000,000. The original-language regression strings remain in tests and the historical source document.\n    39\t\n    40\tThe evaluation asks whether every amount in the judged band satisfies the user's claim (`holds`), none does (`not_yet`), or only some do (`borderline`). An equality claim holds only for a singleton amount. Missing judgement returns `not_judged`; an unassigned boundary is not guessed.\n    41\t\n    42\tHistorical validation covered all 43 money ladders selected independently by text pattern and refused six non-money ladders. Boundary, exact-amount and dateline cases remain regression requirements. That result does not validate newly translated English-only bands; D163 must test those separately.\n    43\t\n    44\t## Search contract\n    45\t\n    46\tThe index embeds each thesis's own text, not its evidence. A content hash detects outdated vectors. Search can report semantic mode only when all relevant candidates have usable current vectors; absent, empty or stale vectors require lexical mode and explicit missing-vector reporting. A second unchanged index build embeds nothing. A failed batch reports already stored progress.\n    47\t\n    48\tD91's null means no recorded coverage, not low relevance. D132 checks named subjects using IDs, full names and configured aliases (including products), the text index, and semantic coverage where enabled. An off-subject similarity floor is not a universal relevance threshold: an irrelevant sentence naming NVDA can score above a relevant narrower query. The caller still judges relevance.\n    49\t\n    50\t`--lexical` disables embedding calls throughout startup and requests. Query vectors are cached by text and model. The historical evaluation checks four example questions within their top three accepted matches, plus day-zero coverage; mapper suggestions are not human gold labels.\n    51\t\n    52\t## State × event and failures\n    53\t\n    54\t| State/event | Required behavior |\n    55\t|---|---|\n    56\t| Historical read | Filter every attached value by its own sequence/day, not only the thesis creation date |\n    57\t| Missing thesis | HTTP 404 or MCP tool error |\n    58\t| Invalid parameter | HTTP 400 |\n    59\t| Corrupt judgement | Raise `LedgerError`; HTTP 500, not fabricated state |\n    60\t| Missing/stale vector | Truthful lexical mode and missing-vector list |\n    61\t| Lexical configuration | No embedder invocation |\n    62\t| Read request | No ledger writes; derived index/cache writes remain separate |\n    63\t| Concurrent HTTP requests | Each request owns its SQLite connections |\n    64\t| Embedding failure | Report status/type without exposing the response body or credentials |\n    65\t\n    66\tNo query opens, closes or rejudges a thesis. No read route stores user commitments or performs trades. The ingestion pipeline remains the ledger writer.\n    67\t\n    68\t## Transports and local operation\n    69\t\n    70\t- `scripts/hivemind_mcp.py` exposes the readers as tools over stdio or streamable HTTP at `/mcp`.\n    71\t- `scripts/hivemind_api.py` is the threaded HTTP front, listening on localhost by default.\n    72\t- Both build required index content at startup unless lexical mode is selected.\n    73\t- `Dockerfile`, `Dockerfile.mcp` and `docker-compose.yml` mount the ledger/items read-only and service index/archive storage read-write. Credentials remain server-side and are never included in a bundle.\n    74\t\n    75\tThe October 24 environment check found both containers could answer local HTTP/MCP reads, but the host VPN did not carry Docker VM traffic for external model calls. The recorded options were host networking or an explicitly configured reachable proxy. This is an environment observation, not a current connectivity claim or authorisation to change networking.\n    76\t\n    77\tThe [README](../../README.md#run-the-service-for-testing) owns executable startup and shutdown instructions. The HTTP default is port 8788. MCP defaults to stdio; port 8789 serves `/mcp` only when streamable HTTP is selected. Use `--lexical --config ''` on the HTTP front for reads without model calls; MCP's read-only tool set needs only `--lexical`. Local source databases are required and are not included in a clone.\n    78\t\n    79\tVerification requirements live in `tests/test_service.py`, `tests/test_search.py` and transport/bundle tests. New language behavior must retain their historical visibility, numeric, cache and failure guarantees. The [UI integration](../../poc/ui/README.md) documents the rendering boundary.\n    80\t\n    81\t## Current library envelope (D275)\n    82\t\n    83\t`GET /library?as_of=YYYY-MM-DD` and MCP `thesis_library` return all dated theses, including closed ones, and stored research groups. They call the same read-only bundle writer as `GET /thesis-bundles` / MCP `thesis_bundles`. The complete read is pinned in SQLite read transactions. No search, model call or ledger write occurs. HTTP bundle/library requests do not open an index or model archive.\n    84\t\n    85\tThe envelope declares `coverage: library` or `coverage: selection`. Group members refer only to theses included in that envelope. Identity fields, all evidence source records, replacement recording dates, dependency resolution history and succession are projected from their existing tables. The UI adapts this into its [versioned contract](../../poc/ui/README.md). Original publication and recording dates are separate; membership and relationship visibility follow recording dates.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^(#|.*D(563|66[5-9]|67[0-9]|68[0-9]|690))|refusal|production'\"' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; nl -ba README.md; rg --files scripts src/hivemind poc/config -g '\"'!**/.mcp.json'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^(#|.*D(563|66[5-9]|67[0-9]|68[0-9]|690))|refusal|production'\"' docs/CURRENT_STATE.md; nl -ba README.md; rg --files scripts src/hivemind poc/config -g '\"'!**/.mcp.json'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"1:# Current state\n26:  They are not integrated with production storage. **Step 4 (judging) has not passed** on the clean path: its ladder step is built and\n30:  to production only with the whole clean path, after Step 4 meets its lines; `tests/test_frozen_clean_step2.py` guards its files. Its three lines were met at the acceptance above (one draw; \"later version\"\n31:  relations barely exercised), and no miss of the seeded end-to-end test (clean 18, 18, 17 against production 17, 15, 16) has\n55:  question, 42 of 65 refusals in six draws, and the re-ask changed the rung 8 times (PRD 11, E4). No change proposed; 18 sends, $0.043.\n73:**Clean Step 1 source investigation (2026-11-07):** A [source-loss census](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-LOSS-CENSUS.md) traces two separate losses in the historical cleaner and records reproducible corpus observations. Offline candidate comparisons expose both recovered content and source-identity or contamination risks. A subsequent [full-page diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-FULL-PAGE-RESULTS.md) completed 102 calls on 17 selected articles and independent source-backed review. It exposes examples of both missing or misattributed source text and model errors that persist despite the right text being present. A Codex–Claude council is reviewing the results and earlier attempts; no source-cleaning candidate is adopted, and the source corpus and production defaults are unchanged.\n75:Alex has authorized correcting the test-source cleaner and test data when their defects are confirmed, while the frozen production configuration and ruler remain intact. The cleaner switched 615 originally fed articles off; a replay reproduces all 615, and direct inspection confirms an actual article excluded because its 9,874-character body was flattened to one line. Other examples include a wrong-site shell, paywall and long challenge code, so the false-exclusion count and a general article-identification repair remain to be established. The [council regroup](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-REGROUP-COUNCIL.md) and [source-preservation design](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PRESERVATION-DESIGN.md) describe an isolated candidate checked against all 34,084 records, with a fake-answer actual-runner handoff and provenance display in the replay renderer. The [five source-control calls](../clean-arch/STEP1-CAPTURE-CONTROLS-RESULTS.md) completed and were source-reviewed; two items contain summary details absent from their attached quotations, and one source-visible risk explanation was not returned. This is selected-case evidence, not a general quality result. Broader source-quality comparison, citation completeness and actual writer integration remain open.\n77:**Clean architecture Step 1 (2026-11-06, isolated work on `clean-arch`):** Alex reopened work with the clean architecture. An experimental prepared-source handoff now preserves the archived page, prior cleaned text, source-role decisions and selected-line mapping without changing the original database; quotations for prepared items must also occur contiguously in the original captured page. Two seven-item runs are available in the [clean architecture PRD](../clean-arch/PRD.md), one in the earlier selection order and one in recorded page-timestamp order. The source selector is not a production service and source ordering is not established event-arrival ordering. The runs expose unresolved semantic problems: citations can omit supporting facts despite an exact location match; model output can change a benchmark into a deployment, reverse a backup relation, omit a named customer or assign an unstated currency. A blinded 84-call check-placement/wording comparison and a 27-call same-article citation-completion diagnostic did not give a clean semantic fix and are not adopted. The latter missed an omitted connectivity condition and a reversed backup relationship, and left six expected evidence reviews unaddressed. A further five-round Codex–Claude council produced a narrowly scoped Step 1 link candidate, independently checked for prompt-contract drift. A six-article, 36-call blind comparison completed; the candidate still produced an explicitly self-contradictory link and both arms consistently omitted the same Kyber report and company response. It is not adopted and has not changed the active PRD prompt. A trace confirms that both the Kyber report and open Kyber thesis were supplied, and the raw model answers omitted the report and denial; the reason is not established. See ../poc/data/clean-step1-kyber-trace-2026-11-06/TRACE.md. An isolated six-call comparison showed that hiding the Kyber thesis did not recover the report or denial in any draw. A second six-call comparison returned them in 3/3 draws with the original Kyber paragraph alone and 0/3 with the full article; length, position and surrounding content changed together, so the reason is not isolated. Excerpt outputs have source-attribution limitations. Neither experiment changed state or established a production fix. (Superseded: the next task then was a whole-article coverage approach; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) See ../clean-arch/STEP1-IMPLEMENTATION-COUNCIL.md. The earlier four-round council agreed that no tested semantic fix is ready; its next investigation is a source-backed review of the 49-item historical run and its 160 evidence records, with overlapping issue flags and separate article-level omissions, then a separate comparison of the seven-item replays. See ../clean-arch/STEP1-COUNCIL-REVIEW.md. Preserve relevant evidence while identifying the next intervention; production/frozen history remains as described below. See [Step 1 run log](../clean-arch/STEP1-RUN-LOG.md) and [English reading page](../clean-arch/architecture.html).\n81:**E1 diagnosed (2026-11-06, Alex chose E1):** of 57 helpful matters frozen production wrote and then dropped, two readers agree on 17 link misses (4 core), 3 opening misses (1 core) and 24 small matters the standard itself drops; 10 are disputed on the fallback clause and 3 were quote refusals. Opening is not the experiment. The core of the link misses is the model reading a question's period, population or named configuration as a boundary; only 8 of the 13 window draws it dropped were lost to the product. No production change; $0.02 of probes. My recommendation was to fold that narrow change into the next extraction version; withdrawn by the seal (D379). Details: `docs/research/2026-11-06-e1-no-home-drops/README.md`, PRD 11 E1.\n83:**Split extraction sealed as a failed experiment (D378, 2026-11-06):** the split (a new-question call, an evidence call and record preparation) was benchmarked against the frozen poc-v108 and failed. Its stop line fired: helpful matters 156, 156, 159 against 166, 170, 162, with about 15% higher cost. One screened revision kept more noise than production. It is sealed in the worktree `hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review` (tag `split-extraction-failed-sealed-2026-11-06`), and nothing of it is in main. What it taught is written as proposed production experiments in `docs/prd/11-production-experiments-from-split.md`: E1 no-home helpful drops, E2 duplicate questions, E3 ladder checks with D186, E4 judge refusals, E5 D203's revision day, E6 the measurement kit. Each opens only on Alex's word.\n93:**Simple new-question diagnostic (2026-11-05):** At Alex's direction, an isolated prompt returned new questions plus article evidence without asking for product database fields. Four previously seen real dev articles each ran once: three returned one candidate, one returned none, all four quotes matched their source, and the gold-listed GPU backstop amount question was not proposed. Whether that omission is a new-topic miss depends on whether the existing library covers the topic. Two other candidates require source and independent-significance review; the actual experimental snapshot already had a Kyber topic, so its pass follows Alex's topic-level rule; whether its exact release question captures the shipping milestone remains unverified. This is not a production or full-pipeline acceptance. Details: `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/simple-new-question-results.zh.md`.\n95:**Split prompt structure v3 read diagnostic (2026-11-05):** One real reading-stage call on a previously seen article found the revenue/margin guidance and Trainium scale but omitted the gold China revenue/market-loss matter. The prewritten stop condition was triggered and the linking call was not sent. This is one draw and not a production or end-to-end result. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v3-read-result.zh.md`. D374 removes the model sampling field from active settings and default configs; historical config files and archives retain their original bytes.\n97:**Split prompt structure v2 small test (2026-11-05):** Thirty calls on two previously seen golden-v5 dev articles completed in `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review`. The blind model reader found complete helpful matters in the fresh control versus new A+B of 6/19 vs 10/19, 11/19 vs 7/19, and 9/19 vs 12/19. The new arm also retained more gold not-kept matters in each draw; B missed useful margin guidance and one answer reused IDs, with a demonstrated matching collision. This is not a production acceptance. See `../hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/docs/research/2026-11-05-parallel-refinement/structure-v2-results.zh.md` and `reports/2026-11-05-parallel-structure-v2.html`.\n103:## Future evidence scoring (D372)\n107:## Latest decision (D369)\n114:**One-day smoke completed (2026-11-05 UTC):** Alex requested a quick 1–2 day end-to-end test. One day, 2026-08-29, ran in `/Users/lchingen/hivemind-no-preseed-evidence-review/poc/data/v117-one-day-smoke-2026-11-05/`: config v117, an 8/28 historical library, 40 extraction calls, 93 evidence records written, four new questions, 54 judged and zero terminal failures. Two judgement attempts were refused and corrected. A Microsoft revenue forecast of $391.1bn was assigned to a rung whose upper bound excludes $391.1bn; the same article's actual quarterly revenue and next-quarter guidance were dropped with no home. Some source dates and bodies are not verified, and an independent 14-article sample review was incomplete due to truncated text. This is not semantic or production acceptance. Findings and scope: `docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/one-day-v117-smoke-2026-11-05.zh.md` in that worktree. The clean source-text comparison and its condition-hidden review are complete; council discussion of next steps is ongoing.\n116:**D371, in progress:** Alex directed that present and forward-looking revenue and financial information should be tracked. Diagnose the same-day extraction and filing losses before designing a general change. No new financial tracking implementation has been accepted yet. The old frozen standard and gold are unchanged. The original prompt-only pilot failed design review. The user approved an exact prompt addition for an experiment; it is not a production implementation. Four paired real-article diagnostic calls per arm completed for the exact approved sentence: v74i versus experimental role-d371-pilot. Microsoft next-quarter guidance and NVIDIA FY2028 guidance still dropped in the treatment; a single draw is not acceptance or proof of causation. Details: docs/research/2026-11-05-real-input-evidence/d371-prompt-pilot-results.zh.md (isolated worktree). The requested full prompt audit and restructure were drafted experimentally; five real-article paired diagnostic requests per arm completed. Microsoft revenue guidance was missed in both arms, and the draft produced unsupported original dates, a quote refusal and suspect links. No production prompt change or acceptance; see restructure-pilot-results.zh.md in the isolated worktree.\n120:**Clean Step 1 coverage order result (2026-11-07):** The isolated 54-call clean comparison completed and all answers were reviewed blind. Moving the evidence-reading block recovered a known Kyber report and denial in one of three candidate answers against none in three controls, with attribution blur; it also produced unsupported gaming-to-AI links in two candidate answers and no controls. It is not adopted. A separate exact-quote example misattributes an executive's prediction in the standalone summary. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-ORDER-RESULTS.md) record all selected cases, uncertainties and source-fidelity work; no production ledger was written.\n122:**Clean Step 1 fidelity council (2026-11-07):** Codex and Claude Code completed five rounds on source attribution, conditions, dates and meaning. No tested fix or root cause is established. The agreed source-to-record review of 49 articles / 160 evidence items and the subsequent isolated field-order comparison are complete; the later 54-answer / 217-record repeated sample remains separate. Prior source-first, quote-first and follow-up-review experiments and their limits are recorded in [the fidelity council](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-COUNCIL.md). No Luna experiment or production change was made in this council.\n124:**Clean Step 1 fidelity review (2026-11-07):** All 49 saved source packets and 160 original evidence items have received a source-and-record review; focused independent cross-checks cover the findings and selected controls. The [review and article replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-REVIEW.md) distinguish source contamination and missing source material from model meaning, citation, date and link issues. The [isolated schema property-order diagnostic](../clean-arch/STEP1-CITATION-ORDER-RESULTS.md) completed all 66 calls and condition-blinded article reviews. Several omissions and semantic errors persisted; it did not establish a fix and is not adopted. It used saved articles and before-states without changing production.\n126:**Clean Step 1 source handoff council (2026-11-07):** The ongoing [council and offline checks](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-HANDOFF-COUNCIL.md) reconstructed 16 matching article-34 controls, all omitting a Kyber report and denial; the cause remains unknown. The 20-article saved source-role overlap review distinguished previously cited AI-generated summary lines and an unrelated recommendation from authored body. The clean runner does not send the saved source-role labels; the council chose to first compare original and previously selected source text on ten known articles. The isolated 60-call source-text comparison and condition-hidden review are complete; [results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-TEXT-RESULTS.md) show specific contamination reductions alongside persistent omissions and changed meaning. It did not advance state or write production. The isolated six-article low-versus-medium source-fidelity diagnostic completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-REASONING-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md) show partial improvements alongside persistent source-qualification and attribution concerns; no production setting is changed. The [prompt audit](../clean-arch/STEP1-FIDELITY-PROMPT-AUDIT.md) maps the observed failures to the full request and signed standard. The six-article isolated schema-description comparison completed 36/36 calls and condition-hidden review; the candidate did not preserve the repeated example qualification and is not adopted. [Results](../clean-arch/STEP1-SUMMARY-FIDELITY-RESULTS.md). A further six-article 36-call low-versus-medium comparison used corpus-cleaned sources outside the original 49-item clean replay and a fixed exploratory context; results were mixed, including unsupported links and incorrect attribution/date fields. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-ADDITIONAL-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). The structured-claims candidate was not sent after review revealed scope and complexity concerns. Council review of the next action is ongoing. The date-text schema-description diagnostic completed 48/48 calls on eight known cases, with mixed results; the candidate is not adopted. A selected-run census found twelve literal \"null\" strings in date-text fields, but no production writer impact is established. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-DESCRIPTION-RESULTS.md). A further preregistered eight-source date comparison completed 48/48 calls; the candidate lost a supported company-article statement date in all three draws on one source and is not adopted. [Results and replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-DATE-NEW-SOURCES-RESULTS.md). A bounded corpus census confirmed 163 source pages across three explicit generated-summary structures whose labels were removed while some summary text was retained; two NVIDIA cases were reproduced through the cleaner. [Source provenance investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). A read-only source-lineage replay reproduced 363/363 cleaned pages with current inputs, preserved ambiguous original-line locations, and surfaced the generated-summary label loss for review. [Source investigation and lineage replay](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-PROVENANCE-CENSUS.md). An offline neighboring-context observation exposed only 144 or 149 of 163 known labels and introduced many other page lines; it is not adopted as a general source fix. [Observations and viewer](../clean-arch/STEP1-SOURCE-CONTEXT-OBSERVATIONS.md). A new [saved-quote integrity investigation](../clean-arch/STEP1-QUOTE-INTEGRITY-CENSUS.md) reconstructed 360 saved answers across eight overlapping cohorts, including 78 prepared-source calls. Five saved unverified passage instances were complete matches under the existing normalization; an in-memory candidate recovered their original slices, while ten others remained unverified. The normalized source-slice lookup is now installed only in the isolated clean Step 1 runner; saved-answer and entry-point checks are recorded there. Page provenance, Unicode completeness, production resource budgets and writer integration remain open. The existing writer stores only one quote, whereas 63 of 160 evidence items in the 49-article exploratory trace have multiple passages. A [source-first 49-article coverage review](../clean-arch/STEP1-COVERAGE-CENSUS.md) separates material missing from the model input from source-visible omissions in the saved answers, with uncertain matter boundaries labeled. (Superseded: the next investigation then was why supplied material is missed; the current next task is under \"One next task\".) The canonical prompt and production configuration remain unchanged. Adding fallible role annotations remains a separate untested candidate. Council streams and handoffs are in `poc/data/clean-step1-ongoing-council-2026-11-07/`.\n128:## What runs\n130:- **Nothing is live.** The two-week run of D563 finished on 2026-11-16 at 01:23 local (`poc/data/clean-carried-run-2026-11-16/`, draw\n140:## Open defects\n159:## One next task\n221:**The choice is made by the council under D439 (D544, 2026-11-15): the work toward production carries forward on the new\n228:answer-choice step has not met its lines, Stage B did not pass, integration is undone, and its cost and time to production are\n246:- **The first blocker (D549):** the step declines some worthwhile questions. From the July refusal review, no general,\n256:refusal) did not repeat. It is evidence, not a gate: it passes nothing and reverses nothing. Traced at $0: the largest reason\n257:for declining over every stored run (22 of 37 refusals) is a topic that asks two things (two years, or two figures), which the\n273:topic's main one (D397, D469, D470); a study of the \"no main part\" refusals found no candidate\n285:different on 3, a question in one run and a refusal in another on 4, refused in both on 1, opened by one run of three on 6.\n310:**Where it stands (2026-11-16; D563 to D567; Alex not active, D439 in force; about $26.07 of D439's $40 spent):**\n311:- **The two-week product-condition run (D563, D564):** read in five ways; gate 1 (Alex's six lines) is not met there either (7 or\n320:  closes due questions with production's closing step, walks calendar days and owns its sequence (D567,\n321:  [design](research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/CLOSING-DESIGN.md)). A production defect found on the way is fixed: a\n332:  [serving](research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/page-contract/SERVE-RESULT.md)). Listed for Alex, not changed: production's Yes/No colours\n355:  - **What it fixed:** no part left unlisted and no refusal on the motivating topics (the control left parts out on 6 topics); every\n356:    refusal control kept.\n433:**The merge test (D678–D680, 2026-11-19): closed; the registered test did not pass.** The old system opens questions, clean Step 1 reads\n446:- **Next:** superseded by D681, below.\n448:**The one next task: none open (D689, D690, 2026-11-20).** poc-v118 is the product (Alex chose A, D690), and the research line\n451:- **D689 (Alex chose option 1; council N closed both lines; record:\n462:  - **Spend (an error of mine).** \"Inside D439's $40\" was written in D679, D682 and D684 with no tally. D439 stands at about $39.65 or\n464:- **History of this line (D681–D688)** follows. Alex opened the hybrid's build and\n465:test, and asked that problems not be found only at the end (his words are in the D681 record). Plan: [PLAN.md](research/2026-11-20-hybrid/PLAN.md). Design:\n479:- **Status (D685):**\n487:- **Opening census (D686):** [OPENING-CENSUS.md](research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md). Of the 16 matters old never opens:\n491:- **D382 answered (D687; his words are in the record):** in the hybrid, a company's own material figure keeps its own question, as\n493:- **Attainable check (D688, council M):** [OPENING-CENSUS.md](research/2026-11-20-hybrid/OPENING-CENSUS.md), last two sections.\n494:  - The census found no credible path to a D665 lead through opening changes alone.\n499:- **D688's recommendation, answered by D689:** keep poc-v118 as production and pause the hybrid's release line. Questions on other\n503:**Earlier next task, closed by D676 and D677 (D665–D675): read the release comparison's six draws when they finish, with the council-reviewed tools**\n504:(`docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md` §1.4–§1.6; `DESIGN-1.6.md` v7; D675).\n510:    round 10; the scorer hardened through Codex rounds 11–21 (D675); 101 stage0 tests, 75 mutants on the reading tools.\n516:  - **Diagnosed (D676, council):** D382's figure exclusion plus the Step 1→ladder bundled-question loss (D432). Even repaired, the new system ties at best. The old system stays; no further comparison run.\n517:  - **The goal loop is closed (D677, council).** The old system stays and no paid work continues. It reopens only if Alex reverses D505\n538:- **If the verdict is not a practical lead:** the council-loop skill on the traces, and the next try from another angle (D674).\n592:controls excluded on the question and the 3 with missing material; the end-to-end gate is against production as it now runs,\n619:  | 4. no refusal without a justified reason | missed, 2 of 17 | missed, 1 failed by both |\n630:- **Stage A does not compare the step with production** (D400). The reference reading of 36 production ladders, other\n667:  readers fail, against the first run: R on 4 ladders (7), N on 0 refusals of 16 (1 of 17), 37 theses right and 32 by a ladder\n708:  whole (18 of 34 as sent), the new refusal firing only on the three theses that are not a year's figure.\n738:  against it (the page fails at 3). It mends what one rule and one field show (the three wrong refusals, a ceiling as a\n739:  reference, a missing unit); it removed two correct references, turned a correct refusal into a ladder and traded one\n783:  run-4 ladders, Stage B judgements) against fresh production draws (`poc-v118`, no seeded questions), on the frozen judgement ruler by one\n788:  reader B on one or two a draw (production 1/1/1, clean 1/2/1; two unresolved each); no eligible judgement disagrees in either; every\n794:  shared spending ledger over every paid call (Step 1, Step 2's embeddings and Jev, the ladder step, the judge, production), the\n797:  outcome of three passes), the judge and production (one day) each sent every network call under a ledger reservation; a ledger\n798:  refusal stops every component; bypass mutants are caught. Full suite 287 passed.\n802:  killed orchestrator handled; full suite 287 passed. Redo of Step 1, Step 2 and production only by a reasoned relaunch; a crash stops\n808:  D453): ledger $11.43, every line attributed (`spend-by-component.json`). Clean 15/10/15 theses, 69/72/92 judgements; production 12/13/14\n811:  census (`CENSUS-FULL-RESULT.md`): settled agreements on the seven graded questions clean 2/1/0, production 1/1/1 (2/2/1 and 2/2/2 with\n813:  questions, production 39 and left kept Kyber and Ohio facts unlinked; Stage B line 3 unexercised (0 settled, at most 4 of 8), its judgement\n815:- **Traced since (D456–D458), all at $0:** production's kept-but-unlinked facts cost no final answer in this window (three mechanisms,\n831:- **Since (D467–D473):** the ladder-cost proofs are kept as tools (`ladder-cost/`); the ladder-refusal candidate stopped on a contested\n832:  \"main figure\" boundary; the 41 Stage A reader splits recorded, none changing its verdict; the ten right-by-refusal theses traced; the period\n837:  first-pass refusals (`reachability/RESULT.md`): six right, four reachable only on paper, one split. **D477:** Stage A not passed; no theme or\n838:  ladder change for the H100 boundary; no more Step 1 sentence changes on these articles; production stays the default; the clean path's\n840:- **Since (D478–D482), under D439:** choice 2 tested and failed (c19); choice 3 taken (production stays the default); production's price\n846:  reading (`LEAN-LOG.md` §2). Slice passed; production's week draw complete; the clean draw stopped twice on dropped connections, root-caused and fixed (D488: Step 1 resends a\n     1\t# Hivemind\n     2\t\n     3\tHivemind reads a stream of information as a hedge-fund analyst would: it identifies theses, links supporting and opposing evidence, and updates its judgement as information arrives. Equities, commodities and cryptocurrencies use the same process.\n     4\t\n     5\tThis repository rebuilds the earlier system in `../tn-hivemind`. It contains a working proof of concept, a browsable thesis library and an Idea explorer. The pipeline runs end to end and has been measured on one company and one 14-day window; it has not passed product acceptance. [Current state](docs/CURRENT_STATE.md) records what runs, the open defects and the one next task; [Decisions](docs/DECISIONS.md) records what is decided now.\n     6\t\n     7\tFor example, an Idea about NVIDIA revenue can draw on several stored theses. The explorer shows the selected theses, their weights, evidence and historical assessments. Its confidence chart applies the selected weights to those assessments; it is not a calibrated probability of the Idea coming true.\n     8\t\n     9\t## Start here\n    10\t\n    11\t| Goal | Start with |\n    12\t|---|---|\n    13\t| Browse the included demo | [Thesis Library and Explore Ideas](poc/mock/index.html); serve it locally using the command below |\n    14\t| Understand the system | [Design page](docs/system-design.html) and [PRD index](docs/prd/README.md) |\n    15\t| Run the API or connect an agent | [Run the service for testing](#run-the-service-for-testing) and [service contract](docs/poc/service.md) |\n    16\t| Run or measure the pipeline | [Run the pipeline](#run-the-pipeline) and the [pipeline guide](docs/poc/pipeline-plan.md) |\n    17\t| Work on the UI | [Frontend integration and development](poc/ui/README.md) |\n    18\t| Work on this repository | [Working rules](AGENTS.md), [current state](docs/CURRENT_STATE.md) and [decisions](docs/DECISIONS.md) |\n    19\t\n    20\tThe UI has two workspaces. **Thesis Library** browses the collected English snapshot and its research groups. **Explore Ideas** submits a question to the API and displays an analysis with weighted theses. Its Trigger compares the displayed confidence with a chosen threshold. Its Action card is an editable preview with a dated NVDA price snapshot; it has no wallet connection or order execution.\n    21\t\n    22\t## Open the demo and design\n    23\t\n    24\tClone the repository, then serve its root. Replace the example path with your checkout's absolute path:\n    25\t\n    26\t```sh\n    27\tgit clone https://github.com/Advent-AI/hivemind.git\n    28\trepo=/absolute/path/to/hivemind\n    29\tpython3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory \"$repo\"\n    30\t```\n    31\t\n    32\tOpen <http://127.0.0.1:8000/poc/mock/index.html> for the app, or <http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs/system-design.html> for the design. Stop the page server with Ctrl-C. The included standalone page can browse its embedded snapshot without the API; submitting an Idea needs the service below.\n    33\t\n    34\tThe design page also opens directly from disk. **New** shows the rebuild and **Old** the historical comparison. Select a module to read its flow, examples and linked PRD. The bundled PRDs keep these links usable without a server.\n    35\t\n    36\t## How it works\n    37\t\n    38\t```text\n    39\tResearch universe: what to research and which information categories to track\n    40\t  ↓ determines what upstream supplies\n    41\tUpstream (outside this system): obtain information and classify it\n    42\t  ↓\n    43\tStart with an empty ledger or a supplied, article-created research library.\n    44\tThe default does not build a cold-start library or import the former preset questions.\n    45\t  ↓\n    46\tEach day, in order:\n    47\t  Dispatch: validate each item, assign a sequence number and route it\n    48\t  Extraction (read-only): read the whole item with the questions open at the start\n    49\t    of the day; return evidence with sources, the questions each statement bears on,\n    50\t    candidate questions and research groups; a statement no question takes is dropped\n    51\t  Thesis library: match each candidate (open, merge, continue a period, or skip) and\n    52\t    each evidence piece (repeat, replacement or new); save matching records for labelling\n    53\t  Judging: once at the end of the day, re-judge the open questions whose evidence\n    54\t    changed, dependencies first\n    55\t  Pull mode: close every question whose due day has come, at the rung it stands on\n    56\t```\n    57\t\n    58\tA thesis has a question, an outcome ladder and a due date, fixed when it is created. An asset thesis concerns a company, cryptocurrency or commodity; its five rungs run from strongly bullish to strongly bearish. A theme thesis concerns macroeconomics, policy or an industry; its rungs name outcomes without judging them as good or bad. A yes/no question has two rungs.\n    59\t\n    60\tThe thesis has one current state: a rung on its ladder. A dependency means one thesis reads another's conclusion when judged. Succession records which period follows which. A research group collects the questions that track one ongoing matter; extraction declares it (D165), and it is distinct from a dependency or duplicate identity.\n    61\t\n    62\tOther modules:\n    63\t\n    64\t- **Config** selects model settings and the number of search results.\n    65\t- **Pull mode** owns time-based work: at the end of each day it closes every question whose due day has come, at the rung it stands on; it re-judges nothing (D356).\n    66\t- **Human review pool** holds unresolved identifiers, proposed themes outside the universe and repeated processing failures.\n    67\t\n    68\tFor example, NVIDIA's April 15, 2025 8-K says H20 exports to China require a licence and may cause a charge of up to $5.5 billion. With an empty library, extraction proposes a theme thesis about licences for the affected chips and an NVDA thesis about FY2026 China revenue versus FY2025. Each carries quotations from the filing. Matching can open both; the licence theme then reaches the revenue thesis through the theme block its judgement reads (D191, D195). AMD's subsequent MI308 filing can contribute to the same licence thesis. A Reuters retelling of the NVIDIA filing adds another source for existing evidence, not new corroboration.\n    69\t\n    70\t## Design and implementation status\n    71\t\n    72\tThe foundational design was frozen on October 22, 2026; later decisions authorize specific changes and the POC build. A design freeze does not imply that the implementation has passed acceptance. Changes to a frozen module require a recorded decision.\n    73\t\n    74\t| Module | Status |\n    75\t|---|---|\n    76\t| Research universe | First version frozen; specific themes remain a draft |\n    77\t| Upstream classification, Config, dispatch | First versions frozen |\n    78\t| Extraction | Frozen at extraction v74i (D274): one decider (D264) files each statement under the questions open at the start of its day; at coverage start each question reads the company's latest earnings report once, when one is found (D363–D365) |\n    79\t| Thesis library, matching and judging | Frozen as the production configuration runs them (D354): Jev matching (D169) with the collision judge (D190); judging-v7 (D263) |\n    80\t| Human review pool | Intake frozen; output not yet designed |\n    81\t| Pull mode and closing at the due day | Decided and built: at the end of each day a due question freezes at its rung and closes; no separate final verdict (D54, D356) |\n    82\t\n    83\tThe replay default is `poc/config/poc-v118.json` (D370); it removes seeded starting questions and the separate coverage-start report import. It has not been accepted end to end. Every measurement uses one frozen ruler: the extraction standard [`docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html`](docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html) (its version 5: version 3 as signed in D243, with D248's and D250's boundaries) with its golden set golden-v5, the seed questions and the judgement gold ([AGENTS §7.1](AGENTS.md); D261, D262). Tests fail when a piece of either moves (`tests/test_frozen_rulers.py`, `tests/test_frozen_extraction_prompt.py`, `tests/test_frozen_questions_and_judgement.py`, `tests/test_baseline.py`). Maintained documentation, new product text and pages written for Alex's review are in English (D163, D368); historical records and quotations keep their original language.\n    84\t\n    85\t## Local requirements and data\n    86\t\n    87\tUse Python 3.12 or later for the backend and Node.js 22 or later with npm for frontend builds. The HTTP service uses the Python standard library. The optional MCP service needs `mcp==1.26.0`. Frontend development dependencies are locked in `poc/ui/package-lock.json`.\n    88\t\n    89\tThe default backend reads these local files:\n    90\t\n    91\t| Purpose | Path |\n    92\t|---|---|\n    93\t| Collected thesis ledger | `poc/data/served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828/views.sqlite` |\n    94\t| Source items | `poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite` (cleaned pages, D351, with the whole pages as fetched in column `text_page`; also kept apart in `items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.page.sqlite`) |\n    95\t| Replay configuration | `poc/config/poc-v118.json` (D370): v108 extraction, matching and judging settings without seeded starting questions or separate report import |\n    96\t| Idea configuration | `poc/config/poc-v118-idea.json`: two discovery calls and a final adoption/weighting call |\n    97\t| Derived service index and Idea archive | `poc/data/service-english/` by default; overridable per run |\n    98\t\n    99\t`poc/data/` is excluded from Git. A fresh clone includes the standalone demo, not these databases. Use the [data manifest](docs/research/2026-10-25-english/data-manifest.md) to locate preserved runs, or supply your own files with the documented CLI options. Keep provider credentials in the owner-only local file `~/.config/hivemind/credentials.json`, outside the repository.\n   100\t\n   101\t### Switching the served data\n   102\t\n   103\tThe page build, the two services and Compose each carry the same two defaults: the thesis ledger (`views.sqlite`) and the items database the ledger's input ids come from. Swap both together; a ledger whose items are missing shows evidence without titles or sources. Serve a copy of a ledger a run may still write (`snapshot.sh` in a run folder makes one with SQLite's backup API), never the file the run writes. The places that name the defaults, all changed in one edit:\n   104\t\n   105\t| Place | What to change |\n   106\t|---|---|\n   107\t| `scripts/mock_build.py` | `--ledger` and `--items` defaults, then `python3 scripts/mock_build.py` rebuilds `poc/mock/index.html` |\n   108\t| `scripts/hivemind_api.py` | `--views` and `--items` defaults |\n   109\t| `scripts/hivemind_mcp.py` | `--views` and `--items` defaults |\n   110\t| `docker-compose.yml` | the `/data/ledger` and `/data/items/items-window.sqlite` volumes of both services |\n   111\t| This README and `poc/ui/README.md` | the default paths named in the table above and in the build description |\n   112\t| `tests/test_ui_bundles.py` | `SERVED_VIEWS` and `SERVED_GROUPS`, the served library's thesis and group counts |\n   113\t\n   114\tThen restart the API in a new dated service folder (below) so the search index is built for the new ledger. The current default, `served-2026-10-28-v34-nvda-3m-through-0828`, is the three-month NVDA run (`poc-v34`, extraction v18) stopped and sealed at 2026-08-28 on evidence bloat (D358); its folder README names the one empty thesis removed.\n   115\t\n   116\t## Run the service for testing\n   117\t\n   118\tRun test services on the host. Start with read-only browsing and lexical search, which need no model or embedding calls. In the same shell where `repo` names the checkout, create a fresh dated run directory for the log, PID and derived files:\n   119\t\n   120\t```sh\n   121\tservice_run=$(mktemp -d \"$repo/poc/data/service-$(date +%F)-XXXXXX\")\n   122\tnohup python3 \"$repo/scripts/hivemind_api.py\" --port 8788 \\\n   123\t  --lexical --config '' \\\n   124\t  --index \"$service_run/search-index.sqlite\" \\\n   125\t  --archive \"$service_run/idea-archive.sqlite\" \\\n   126\t  > \"$service_run/api.log\" 2>&1 &\n   127\tprintf '%s\\n' \"$!\" > \"$service_run/api.pid\"\n   128\tcurl -fsS http://127.0.0.1:8788/days\n   129\t```\n   130\t\n   131\t`/days` returns the first and last completed ledger day. See the log if startup is not ready. To enable Idea submission and semantic search, stop this instance and start the same command without `--lexical --config ''`. That selects the default Idea configuration above and can call the embedding and language models. Exact archived requests can replay without another model call; changed requests can incur cost.\n   132\t\n   133\tThe app calls `http://127.0.0.1:8788`; its host may override this with `<body data-api=\"…\">`. `/library` returns the dated snapshot. `--views` and `--items` select other source databases; `--index`, `--archive` and `--config` select derived storage and configuration. Stop only the API instance you started:\n   134\t\n   135\t```sh\n   136\tkill \"$(cat \"$service_run/api.pid\")\"\n   137\t```\n   138\t\n   139\tFor an agent, `scripts/hivemind_mcp.py` defaults to stdio. Use `--transport streamable-http --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8789` to serve `/mcp`, and `--lexical` for model-free reads. The [service contract](docs/poc/service.md) lists the tools and historical-read rules.\n   140\t\n   141\tDocker is an alternative when its outbound networking works. Compose mounts the source ledger/items read-only, `poc/data/service-english/` read-write, and credentials read-only. Prepare the MCP wheels on the host before building:\n   142\t\n   143\t```sh\n   144\tpython3 -m pip download mcp==1.26.0 --dest build/wheels --only-binary=:all: --platform manylinux2014_aarch64 --platform manylinux_2_17_aarch64 --platform any --python-version 3.12 --implementation cp --abi cp312 --abi abi3 --abi none   # once, for the MCP image\n   145\tdocker compose up --build -d\n   146\tdocker compose ps && curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8788/days\n   147\tdocker compose down\n   148\t```\n   149\t\n   150\tThe recorded VPN setup on this Mac blocked outbound model calls from Docker; host operation is the tested workflow, and container connectivity needs a fresh check before a container model run.\n   151\t\n   152\t## Run the pipeline\n   153\t\n   154\tReplay writes a new thesis ledger and model-call archive. Before a paid run, replay the relevant saved answers through the current checks at no model cost:\n   155\t\n   156\t```sh\n   157\tpython3 \"$repo/scripts/preflight.py\" \\\n   158\t  --archive /absolute/path/to/preserved/archive.sqlite \\\n   159\t  --views /absolute/path/to/preserved/views.sqlite \\\n   160\t  --items \"$repo/poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite\"\n   161\t```\n   162\t\n   163\tInspect changed refusals before continuing. A replay is a paid run: it starts only when authorized, in a new dated folder, with the agreed spending cap. The default starts without seeded questions and can read an existing article-created library:\n   164\t\n   165\t```sh\n   166\tpython3 \"$repo/scripts/replay.py\" --from 2026-07-01 --until 2026-07-14 --only NVDA --cap 3 \\\n   167\t  --views /absolute/path/to/new-run/views.sqlite --archive /absolute/path/to/new-run/archive.sqlite\n   168\t```\n   169\t\n   170\t`--cap` is checked after each item: parallel calls already sent and the day's judging round can exceed it, so it is not a hard whole-run limit. The default is `poc-v118`; the default input database is `poc/data/items-nvda-2026-07-01-10-27.sqlite`. A ledger containing the retired seeded questions is refused. Earnings releases still enter as actual source items; a report is not appended as background to other items. The new replay default is not accepted end to end. The historical seeded judgement scorer requires its frozen seeds and therefore is not a valid scorer for the new no-seed run. The [pipeline guide](docs/poc/pipeline-plan.md) describes the stages.\n   171\t\n   172\t\n   173\t## Build and check\n   174\t\n   175\tFrom the checkout root:\n   176\t\n   177\t```sh\n   178\tnpm ci --prefix poc/ui\n   179\tpython3 scripts/mock_build.py\n   180\tnpm --prefix poc/ui run lint\n   181\tnpm --prefix poc/ui run format:check\n   182\tnpm --prefix poc/ui test\n   183\tnpm --prefix poc/ui run test:mutations\n   184\tPYTHONPATH=src:poc python3 -m pytest tests -q\n   185\tpython3 docs/check.py\n   186\t```\n   187\t\n   188\tInstall pytest in your Python environment to run the Python tests. `mock_build.py` reads the local databases and embeds the dated market snapshot into `poc/mock/index.html`; it makes no model calls. `npm --prefix poc/ui run build` builds component assets only. The [frontend guide](poc/ui/README.md) covers the frontend in detail.\n   189\t\n   190\tThe tests make no model calls: `tests/conftest.py` refuses every credential read. `docs/check.py` checks generated blocks, glossary controls, classification simulation and page rendering, and rebuilds the PRD bundle. A pass does not replace the reasoning review in [the PRD index](docs/prd/README.md).\n   191\t\n   192\t## Repository map\n   193\t\n   194\t| Path | Purpose |\n   195\t|---|---|\n   196\t| `AGENTS.md` | Working rules: authority, diagnosing, design, code, tests, verification, writing prompts, measuring and iterating; `CLAUDE.md` points to it |\n   197\t| `docs/DECISIONS.md` | What is decided now, by module, and every decision number's status |\n   198\t| `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` | The full record of every decision, in number order |\n   199\t| `docs/CURRENT_STATE.md` | What runs, open defects and the one next task |\n   200\t| `docs/system-design.html`, `docs/system-design/new-*.js` | Maintained design page and its source data |\n   201\t| `docs/prd/` | One specification per module and an index; `05-extraction-standard.html` is the extraction step's standard (signed, D243) |\n   202\t| `docs/poc/` | The pipeline guide and the service, Idea card and UI contracts |\n   203\t| `docs/check.py` | Mechanical design checks and PRD bundle refresh |\n   204\t| `docs/research/` | Measurements, golds and reviews behind the decisions, by date; `2026-10-29-ledger-standard/` holds the standard's drafts and golden set |\n   205\t| `docs/archive/` | Frozen design snapshots |\n   206\t| `src/hivemind/`, `scripts/`, `poc/` | Implementation, entry points and the POC corpus, configurations and universe |\n   207\t| `poc/eval/gold/`, `poc/reports/` | Earlier golds and the measured reports behind the build log |\n   208\t| `reports/`, `research_notes/` | Research and reviews behind the design |\nsrc/hivemind/vocabulary.py\nscripts/preflight.py\nscripts/review_english_run.py\nscripts/hivemind_api.py\nscripts/report.py\nscripts/idea_stability.py\nscripts/hivemind_mcp.py\nscripts/compare_ledgers.py\nscripts/idea_eval.py\nscripts/idea_gold.py\nscripts/snapshot_store.py\nscripts/score_admission_gold.py\nscripts/idea_probe.py\nscripts/replay.py\nscripts/show_views.py\nscripts/cost_report.py\nscripts/mock_translate.py\nscripts/mock_build.py\nscripts/mock_data.py\nscripts/score_evidence_gold.py\nscripts/trace_items.py\nscripts/agent_day0.py\nscripts/recall_oracle.py\nscripts/search_eval.py\nscripts/benchmark_states.py\nscripts/prompt_size.py\npoc/config/poc-v116.json\npoc/config/poc-v2.json\npoc/config/poc-v117.json\npoc/config/poc-v118-from-v1.json\npoc/config/poc-v118-from-v7.json\npoc/config/poc-v7.json\npoc/config/poc-v11.json\npoc/config/poc-v22.json\npoc/config/poc-v26-idea.json\npoc/config/poc-v118-from-v2.json\npoc/config/poc-v32-idea.json\npoc/config/poc-v108.json\npoc/config/poc-v9.json\npoc/config/poc-v30-idea.json\npoc/config/idea-composite-trial-2026-10-26.json\npoc/config/poc-v118.json\npoc/config/poc-v118-idea.json\npoc/config/poc-v1.json\npoc/config/poc-v33-idea.json\npoc/config/poc-v31-idea.json\npoc/config/poc-v118-from-v9.json\npoc/config/poc-v13.json\nsrc/hivemind/__init__.py\nsrc/hivemind/idea_composite.py\nsrc/hivemind/dispatch.py\nsrc/hivemind/baseline.py\nsrc/hivemind/config.py\nsrc/hivemind/pull.py\nsrc/hivemind/jev.py\nsrc/hivemind/service.py\nsrc/hivemind/seeding.py\nsrc/hivemind/bundle.py\nsrc/hivemind/live_lock.py\nsrc/hivemind/extraction.py\nsrc/hivemind/testdb.py\nsrc/hivemind/universe.py\nsrc/hivemind/replay.py\nsrc/hivemind/research_context.py\nsrc/hivemind/parallel_day.py\nsrc/hivemind/model.py\nsrc/hivemind/search.py\nsrc/hivemind/placement.py\nsrc/hivemind/idea.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/identity.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/jev_matching.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/round.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/matching.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/judging.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/groups.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/__init__.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/writing.py\nsrc/hivemind/views/store.py\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/role-v1.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/categories-v9.json\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/role-v2.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v8.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/judging-v3.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/review-v74i.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/judging-v1.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/categories-v2.json\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-v8.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/role-v74i.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/judging-v7.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-v9.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/matching-v3.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/baseline-v3.json\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/matching-v2.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-v7.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/categories-v1.json\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/matching-v1.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/collision-v1.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/role-v29.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/collision-v2.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/categories-v74i.json\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v6.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/role-v9.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v7.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v5.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v4.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-v10.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-v11.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v1.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v3.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-v12.txt\nsrc/hivemind/prompts/idea-summary-v2.txt\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md; rg -n '\"'^#|v118|cost|seconds|refus|32 matters|D689|D690'\"' docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/{RUN-LOG.md,RUN-PLAN-v12.md}; rg -n '\"'^#{1,5} .*D(689|690)|''^.*D(689|690)'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/live-service/OPERATING-CONTRACT.md; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-16.md; rg -n '\"'^#|v118|cost|seconds|refus|32 matters|D689|D690'\"' docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/{RUN-LOG.md,RUN-PLAN-v12.md}; rg -n '\"'^#{1,5} .*D(689|690)|''^.*D(689|690)'\"' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -20\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# A live new-system store: the operating contract, version 9, agreed for its $0 proof build (D593; nothing built)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status (D593):** agreed by the council in round 119 for a $0 proof build on test stores, after nine versions (rounds 111 to\n     4\t119). That agreement covers test stores only, not live operation; P1 stays a proposed decision. D575 paused the earlier design (`DESIGN.md`) as recorded input; it was not accepted. Round 110 resumed the work as this\n     5\t$0 contract. Version 2 answered round 111's six findings; version 3 answered round 112's four; version 4 answered round 113's five; version 5 answered round 114's four; version 6 answered round 115's four; version 7 answered round 116's three; version 8 answered round 117's two; version 9 answers round 118's two. It returns to the council before any build or live run. The new system has\n     6\tnot passed its quality gates, and adopting it is Alex's choice. This contract only states what a live store would have to guarantee.\n     7\t\n     8\t**Falsifier (round 110):** a required operating case that cannot be served consistently under this contract without blocking the\n     9\twriter's commits or exposing partial work. The proof plan names one test per case, and one failing test sends the contract back.\n    10\t\n    11\t## What touches a live store (searched in the code)\n    12\t\n    13\t- **The writer:** the new system's daily loop through `clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/ladder_adapter.py`.\n    14\t  - It opens the store with `store.open_db`, today in SQLite's default rollback-journal mode.\n    15\t  - It commits several times within a day; `close_and_complete` closes due questions, records the day in `days_completed` and\n    16\t    commits.\n    17\t  - **A stopped run is never resumed** (the module's docstring; `run_stage_b.py` records a crash as the folder's outcome).\n    18\t\n    19\t| Toucher | How | When |\n    20\t|---|---|---|\n    21\t| API (`scripts/hivemind_api.py`) | `Connections`: one read-only connection per request; `search.ensure_index(ledger, index)` at start, which embeds view text through an outside service (row 4a) | every request; once at start |\n    22\t| MCP server (`scripts/hivemind_mcp.py`) | one read-only connection at start (`LEDGER`), shared by every call, including overlapping calls under streamable HTTP; `ensure_index` and the object list computed at start | the whole process |\n    23\t| The page | only through the API | — |\n    24\t| The page's embedded snapshot (`scripts/mock_build.py` → `bundle.library`) | `service.open_ledger` | one build |\n    25\t| Idea and evaluation scripts (`idea_probe`, `idea_gold`, `idea_stability`, `agent_day0`, `search_eval`) | `open_read_only` / `open_ledger` | one run |\n    26\t| Research and census scripts (`docs/research/**`) | `open_read_only` | one run |\n    27\t| Preflight (`scripts/preflight.py`) | **`shutil.copy` of `views.sqlite`** to a scratch file (row 7: snapshot or finished store only, then the backup API) | before a paid run |\n    28\t| A run's `snapshot.sh` (a per-run file, not a repository script) | SQLite's backup API | on demand |\n    29\t\n    30\t## The contract\n    31\t\n    32\t1. **Journal mode, side files, opening.**\n    33\t   - The live store is in WAL mode, set by the writer when it creates the store. Production's stores stay as they are.\n    34\t   - The `-wal` and `-shm` files sit beside the store on a local disk. **No reader reaches a live store through a container bind mount\n    35\t     or a network share.**\n    36\t   - **Opening precondition:** every reader runs as the writer's user, and the store's directory is writable by it. SQLite needs the\n    37\t     `-wal` and `-shm` files readable, or permission to create them, to open a WAL store with `mode=ro`.\n    38\t   - Readers open with `mode=ro`, never `immutable=1`.\n    39\t   - A reader that cannot open the store, or cannot begin a read transaction, fails loudly with the store's path. It never serves an\n    40\t     empty or stale answer in its place. In code (slice 5, D603), `service.open_pinned` raises `StoreUnreadable` for a SQLite failure\n    41\t     while opening, at `BEGIN` or at the pinning read; a later SQLite error is a separate path, not covered by this rule's tests.\n    42\t   - Readers set an explicit busy timeout (`store.READ_BUSY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, 5 s). SQLite's busy handler retries `SQLITE_BUSY` within\n    43\t     it; there is no application-level retry (slice 5, D603). SQLite documents rare WAL cases where a read returns busy, for example\n    44\t     while the WAL index is being rebuilt. The test holds the store with a connection in exclusive locking mode as a stand-in for\n    45\t     them; those cases themselves are not exercised.\n    46\t   - The guarantee is that readers do not block the writer's commits, not that the writer never waits.\n    47\t2. **One committed state per request, one connection per concurrent request** (detailed in council rounds 125 and 126).\n    48\t   - **Pinned, not only begun:** each API request and each MCP tool call opens its connections and runs `BEGIN`, then a first read,\n    49\t     which in WAL mode fixes the committed state the later reads see. The bound resolution is that first read.\n    50\t     - The transaction always ends (`finally`).\n    51\t     - A failure while opening (for example the items or index file) closes whatever was already opened before the error leaves\n    52\t       `__enter__`, since `__exit__` is not called then.\n    53\t   - **MCP opens its ledger, items and index connections per tool call,** as the API does per request, in place of today's\n    54\t     process-wide `LEDGER`, `ITEMS` and `INDEX`.\n    55\t     - The index is written during a search (the query-embedding cache, `query_vectors`), so overlapping searches must not share a\n    56\t       connection.\n    57\t   - **MCP's object list stays in its server instructions, labelled with its day.**\n    58\t     - It is computed once at start-up through `idea.object_list`, which resolves its bound; the direct `service.default_as_of` call\n    59\t       in `scripts/hivemind_mcp.py` goes.\n    60\t     - The instructions name the day: \"objects as of <day>, when this server started; every tool reads the current last completed\n    61\t       day\".\n    62\t     - On a first-day store the instructions say no day has completed yet, and every tool refuses until one has.\n    63\t     - No new tool is added, so the public tool list is unchanged.\n    64\t3. **Every date bound, resolved in one function.**\n    65\t   - Today 13 places resolve a bound as `x or default_as_of(conn)`: `service.py` ×9 (`summaries`, `views`, `view`, `evidence`,\n    66\t     `history`'s `to_day`, `record`, `changes`' `to_day`, `evaluate`, …), `bundle.py` ×2, `idea.py` ×1 and `search.py` ×1. An\n    67\t     explicit date bypasses any check placed in `default_as_of`.\n    68\t   - **The owner:** one function, `service.resolve_bound(conn, bound)`, receives every upper bound, explicit or omitted, in its\n    69\t     caller's read transaction.\n    70\t     - An omitted bound gives the last completed day.\n    71\t     - An explicit bound later than the last completed day is refused, naming that day.\n    72\t     - An explicit bound on or before it passes.\n    73\t   - Every one of the 13 sites calls it. A lower bound (`from_day`, `since_day`) is checked only against its resolved upper bound,\n    74\t     through `resolve_range` (see the section on row 3's product-facing contract).\n    75\t   - **A structural check** fails the suite when any module calls `default_as_of` outside `resolve_bound`, or resolves a bound by\n    76\t     `or`. A behaviour test per caller gives each an explicit future bound and an omitted one.\n    77\t4. **The first day** (open defect 10).\n    78\t   - Before any day completes, every request and tool call is refused, including the streamed Idea route before any header.\n    79\t   - An empty store (no step) and a legacy ledger read as today.\n    80\t4a. **Search: visibility before ranking and limits; an index of text-keyed vectors refreshed by the service at start** (revised in\n    81\t    council rounds 127 and 128).\n    82\t   - **Lexical:**\n    83\t     - `search.search` ranks the visible candidates' texts (from `service.summaries` at the request's bound) in an in-memory\n    84\t       full-text table with the same trigram tokenizer;\n    85\t     - its statistics are those of the visible library only, so a search at the last completed day equals the same search on that\n    86\t       day's backup;\n    87\t     - `store.search_views`, which ranks the whole `views_fts`, stays unchanged for its one other caller, production's matching\n    88\t       (`views/matching.py`), whose behaviour this slice does not touch.\n    89\t   - **The index holds vectors keyed by (view, the text's sha256, the embedding model).**\n    90\t     - A vector is used only when all of these hold (`fresh_vectors`, today's check): the sha covers the **complete embedding\n    91\t       input** (`view_text`: object, kind, what it looks at, the question and every rung's condition), the model matches, the vector\n    92\t       is usable, and its length matches the others'.\n    93\t     - Under those conditions a vector is correct whatever store it was built from.\n    94\t     - Search uses a vector only for a view visible at the request's bound. **The check, the length test included, runs over the\n    95\t       request's visible candidates only** (round 129). Today `fresh_vectors` walks every view and takes the length from the first\n    96\t       usable vector, so an invisible view met first could get a visible view's vector rejected. `fresh_vectors` is given the\n    97\t       visible candidates.\n    98\t     - **The store identity of rounds 115 to 117 is therefore not needed and is dropped.**\n    99\t       - A replaced or restored store can at worst find a view without a matching vector. That view counts as unindexed and search\n   100\t         runs lexically, saying so, as today.\n   101\t       - It can never be given a wrong vector. The proof keeps the replaced-store and restored-store cases to show this.\n   102\t   - **Building:**\n   103\t     - `build_index` embeds only the views visible at the last completed day (resolved by `resolve_bound`), so a view of an\n   104\t       unfinished day is never embedded and never sent to the embedding service;\n   105\t     - each batch commits as today. A failure leaves the vectors already stored, all still correct by their keys, and the next\n   106\t       build embeds the rest;\n   107\t     - no published day is needed, because correctness is per vector;\n   108\t     - `build_index` keeps its signature (`scripts/search_eval.py` and the tests call it as today).\n   109\t   - **What a search sees of the index:**\n   110\t     - the ledger transaction does not cover the index, a separate file;\n   111\t     - search reads every stored view vector in **one statement** (`fresh_vectors`), so the set of vectors a search uses comes from\n   112\t       one committed index state;\n   113\t     - a build committing during the request can change only later reads, and search makes none of view vectors;\n   114\t     - the query cache is keyed by the query's sha and model: a cache hit reuses the stored vector for that exact query and model;\n   115\t       a miss makes an embedding call and stores its answer. No claim is made that a fresh call would return the identical vector.\n   116\t   - **Who refreshes, and when:**\n   117\t     - the service (API and MCP) builds the index at start, as today, to the last completed day; on a first-day store it does not\n   118\t       build;\n   119\t     - after a later day completes, a newly visible view is served one of two ways until the service is restarted:\n   120\t       - **a matching vector is already in the index** (the same view, text and model, from an earlier build or another store\n   121\t         sharing the index): it is used, being correct by its key;\n   122\t       - **no matching vector:** search runs lexically and lists the view in `missing_vectors`, as it does today.\n   123\t     - **A service started on a first-day store** builds no index, and does not build one when that day completes. When that day\n   124\t       completes, its search is semantic only if every visible candidate already has a matching vector in the shared index;\n   125\t       otherwise it is lexical, listing the views without one, until the service is restarted. Search's rule is unchanged: one missing\n   126\t       vector makes the search lexical. This holds for the API and MCP alike.\n   127\t     - The writer never touches the index.\n   128\t     - An automatic refresh while serving is not built; it is a stated limit.\n   129\t\n   130\t5. **Checkpoints and disk: what is guaranteed, and the disk-exhaustion behaviour.**\n   131\t   - The writer sets `journal_size_limit` (proposed 64 MB) and keeps SQLite's automatic passive checkpoint.\n   132\t   - After each completed day it asks for a truncating checkpoint with a short busy timeout.\n   133\t   - **Guaranteed:**\n   134\t     - readers do not block the writer's commits;\n   135\t     - after a completed day whose truncating checkpoint succeeds, the `-wal` is within the limit;\n   136\t     - if the truncating checkpoint fails on 3 consecutive completed days, the writer stops before the next day with a recorded\n   137\t       reason.\n   138\t   - **Not guaranteed:** a byte bound on the `-wal` within an unfinished day, or across the failed days. Its growth there is the\n   139\t     day's own writes, which the contract does not bound.\n   140\t   - **Disk:**\n   141\t     - before each day the writer checks the free space on the store's disk against a minimum stated in the run's limits file\n   142\t       (proposed: 20 times the store's size), and stops before the day with a recorded reason if it is below;\n   143\t     - a write that still meets a full filesystem mid-day (extending the store, its `-wal` or a side file) fails, and the writer\n   144\t       exits non-zero as in a crash (row 6);\n   145\t     - its stop reason may then be lost too, if the run's log is on the full disk;\n   146\t     - **the guarantee in that case is the readers' boundedness** (they keep the last completed day), not the record.\n   147\t       The process's exit status, seen by its supervisor, is the signal.\n   148\t6. **A crash.**\n   149\t   - WAL drops the unfinished transaction. The day's **already committed writes stay** in the store.\n   150\t   - Readers stay bounded to the last completed day. Every row written during the unfinished day is dated by that day's processing\n   151\t     steps, after the last completed day, so it is outside every allowed bound.\n   152\t   - **The writer does not resume** (today's loop). The store is served as of its last completed day until it is replaced.\n   153\t   - Resuming a stopped live store is a separate design, not covered here.\n   154\t7. **Who is writing: a signal outside the store, and copies.**\n   155\t   - **The lock folder: one owner, fixed, fail closed.**\n   156\t     - One function (`live_lock.lock_folder()`) gives the folder to both the writer and preflight: `~/.hivemind-locks/`,\n   157\t       created on first use with owner-only permissions (0700).\n   158\t     - If it cannot be created, opened or written, both refuse and name it. Neither falls back to running without a lock.\n   159\t     - **Lock files are never deleted or replaced** by any part of the system; only a person removes them, by hand, when no process\n   160\t       runs.\n   161\t     - After taking a lock, and again at each later check point (the writer before each day; preflight after its copy), the holder\n   162\t       checks that the lock file's path still names the inode it locked. If not (someone replaced the file by hand), it stops or\n   163\t       discards and refuses.\n   164\t     - A hand deletion while a lock is held is detected at the next check point, not prevented: the rule against deleting lock\n   165\t       files is what prevents it.\n   166\t   - **The lock, named by the store file, not its path:** `<device>-<inode>.writer.lock` in that folder. Both numbers are read with\n   167\t     `stat`, which reads no content of the store.\n   168\t   - **One name per store:**\n   169\t     - SQLite finds a WAL store's `-wal` and `-shm` by the path it opens, so a store must be opened by its own path;\n   170\t     - a symlink is resolved to that path (`realpath`) before anything else;\n   171\t     - a store file with more than one hard link (`st_nlink` > 1) is refused, naming the reason. A hard link elsewhere would open the\n   172\t       main file without its WAL and read a stale state.\n   173\t   - **Operating precondition: a store's path is never replaced in place while it may be in use.** Replacing a store means a new\n   174\t     path. This is the contract's own precondition, supported by AGENTS §9's working rule that \"new stored data gets a new, dated\n   175\t     name\" (round 119), not that rule's wording.\n   176\t   - **The checks detect a single replacement:**\n   177\t     - the holder reads the store's device and inode, takes their lock, opens the store, and reads its path's device and inode\n   178\t       again;\n   179\t     - preflight checks once more after its copy;\n   180\t     - any difference discards what was opened or copied, releases and refuses.\n   181\t   - **What they cannot detect, stated:** a path swapped away and back (A, then B at the moment of the open, then A again) passes\n   182\t     both checks. The contract does not claim to bind the opened file to the locked one in that case; the rule above excludes it.\n   183\t   - **The writer locks before it writes:**\n   184\t     - it creates its store file empty and exclusively (create-new, failing if the path exists);\n   185\t     - it takes that file's lock without waiting;\n   186\t     - it opens it as a database and verifies its identity as above, and only then writes;\n   187\t     - if the lock is held (by preflight), it refuses to start, with that reason.\n   188\t     - It holds the lock (`flock`) for its whole life; the operating system releases it on any exit, crash included.\n   189\t   - **Preflight, before it touches a store, always takes that store's lock without waiting,** creating the lock file if it is\n   190\t     absent. There is no \"no lock file\" branch:\n   191\t     - **if the lock is held, a writer is running:** preflight refuses, naming the run's snapshot script (AGENTS §9's sanctioned way);\n   192\t     - otherwise it keeps the lock through its verified copy (the backup API, from a `mode=ro` source) and releases it after;\n   193\t     - historical stores and production's are copied the same way; taking a fresh lock on them is harmless.\n   194\t     - AGENTS §9 remains the only protection for production's own writer, which takes no such lock; changing it is out of scope.\n   195\t   - **Snapshots:** the snapshot script is the one toucher that copies a store while its writer runs, with the backup API.\n   196\t     Preflight accepts its snapshots like any store with no writer.\n   197\t   - **A copy leaves WAL mode** (found in slice 3's build):\n   198\t     - a backup of a WAL store keeps WAL mode;\n   199\t     - a read-only reader of that copy then fails with \"unable to open database file\", since it cannot create the shared-memory\n   200\t       file;\n   201\t     - so the snapshot script, and preflight's own copy, set `journal_mode=delete` on the copy before anyone reads it.\n   202\t   - **Orderly stop:**\n   203\t     - at its last day the writer tries to leave the store in rollback-journal mode (`journal_mode=delete`, with a short busy\n   204\t       timeout);\n   205\t     - if service readers hold it, the store stays in WAL mode and the writer records \"stopped in order, WAL kept\" in its run's\n   206\t       outcome;\n   207\t     - either way it then releases its lock.\n   208\t     - Journal mode is never used as the sign of a live store; the lock is.\n   209\t8. **AGENTS §9 (\"Never open a live run's databases\") stays in force.**\n   210\t   - **Proposed decision P1, to be resolved before any live-service operation and not assumed by any proof:** the product's API and\n   211\t     MCP server may read a live WAL store under rows 1 to 7. Every other toucher above uses a backup-API snapshot.\n   212\t   - The proofs run on test stores made for them, never on a live run.\n   213\t\n   214\t## State × event (the store's state at the request's transaction × the request)\n   215\t\n   216\t| State | Bounds omitted | Upper bound ≤ last completed day | Upper bound > last completed day | Start-up reads | Copy |\n   217\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   218\t| Empty store | as today | as today | as today | as today | consistent |\n   219\t| First day in progress | refused (4) | — | refused (4) | wait; requests refused | consistent (an in-progress copy, read under the same rules) |\n   220\t| A later day in progress | the last completed day | that bound | refused (3) | the last completed day | consistent |\n   221\t| A day completing during a request | the request's own transaction | same | same | same | consistent |\n   222\t| Crashed mid-day, not resumed | the last completed day | that bound | refused (3) | the last completed day | consistent |\n   223\t| `-wal`/`-shm` absent and directory not writable | fails loudly (1) | same | same | fails loudly | — |\n   224\t\n   225\t## Proof plan ($0, on test stores made for it; nothing built until the council accepts the contract)\n   226\t\n   227\t1. **WAL mode:** the writer's store is in WAL mode with the size limit set; production's stores are unchanged.\n   228\t2. **Every upper bound:**\n   229\t   - the structural check that no bound is resolved outside `resolve_bound`;\n   230\t   - for each of the 13 callers, an explicit future bound is refused naming the last completed day, an explicit past bound passes,\n   231\t     and an omitted bound gives the last completed day;\n   232\t   - lower bounds are unaffected.\n   233\t3. **One state per request:** an API request, its state pinned by its first read, held between its first and last read while the writer commits a judgement and\n   234\t   completes a day returns the state at its start, and the writer's commits succeed. Run on a first-day store and a store with one\n   235\t   completed day.\n   236\t4. **Start-up and MCP concurrency:**\n   237\t   - two overlapping MCP calls across a writer's commit each return their own transaction's state, two overlapping **search**\n   238\t     calls included (each with its own index connection, both writing the query cache);\n   239\t   - after a later day completes, a tool call on the actual MCP entry point sees that day's views; the instructions still name their\n   240\t     start day;\n   241\t   - an error while opening connections leaves none open;\n   242\t   - a call that fails mid-read leaves no open transaction and no open connection;\n   243\t   - a service started on a first-day store refuses requests and builds its index after the first day completes;\n   244\t   - MCP's object list after a later day includes that day's objects;\n   245\t   - no transaction is open between MCP calls.\n   246\t5. **Checkpoint and disk:**\n   247\t   - below the free-space minimum, the writer stops before the day with its reason, and readers serve the last completed day;\n   248\t   - a database-full error mid-day (a test store with `max_page_count`) stops the writer as a crash, and readers are unaffected;\n   249\t   - a filesystem-full error mid-day: the store on a small disk image (`hdiutil`, a few MB), filled while the `-wal` grows. The\n   250\t     writer exits non-zero, and readers serve the last completed day. Whether its reason was recorded is reported, not required;\n   251\t   - with a long read held, the writer commits;\n   252\t   - after the read ends, a checkpoint completes, and a reset brings the `-wal` within the limit;\n   253\t   - with a reader held across three completed days, the writer stops before the fourth with its reason recorded, and readers serve\n   254\t     the last completed day.\n   255\t6. **Opening precondition:** with `-wal`/`-shm` absent and the directory read-only, a reader fails loudly. A busy read is retried\n   256\t   within the timeout.\n   257\t7. **A crash:** the writer is killed after committed writes mid-day.\n   258\t   - Every route's answer, at every allowed bound, equals the answer from a backup taken at the last completed day.\n   259\t   - The search index and the text sent to the embedding service (recorded by a fake transport) equal the backup's.\n   260\t   - The partial day's rows are present in the file and invisible to every route and to the index.\n   261\t8. **Copies:**\n   262\t   - a backup taken while the writer commits passes an integrity check;\n   263\t   - preflight refuses a store whose writer holds the lock, opened by its own path or by a symlink to it;\n   264\t   - a store with a hard link elsewhere is refused by every entry point;\n   265\t   - a crashed writer's store with committed data still in its WAL: a copy through a symlink equals a copy through the own path;\n   266\t   - preflight arriving just before the writer: whichever takes the lock first wins. A writer finding preflight's lock refuses to\n   267\t     start and writes nothing; preflight finding the writer's lock refuses to copy;\n   268\t   - the store's path replaced after preflight's `stat` and before its open, and again during its copy: each is detected, the\n   269\t     copy discarded and the request refused;\n   270\t   - the writer's path replaced between its create and its open: the writer refuses;\n   271\t   - the lock folder unwritable: both refuse and name it;\n   272\t   - a held lock file deleted and recreated by hand: a second process can lock the new file, so exclusion is broken until the\n   273\t     holder's next check. The writer rechecks its lock file's identity before each day and stops if it changed; preflight rechecks\n   274\t     after its copy and discards it. The test shows detection at those points, not prevention;\n   275\t   - it accepts a crashed store (lock released by the operating system), a store stopped in order with its WAL kept, a snapshot,\n   276\t     and every historical input it accepts today;\n   277\t   - a writer started while preflight holds the lock waits or refuses, and never writes under the copy.\n   278\t8a. **Lexical search under an unfinished day:** a paused writer and a crashed one, each with more than 50 unfinished views matching a\n   279\t   query. The search at the last completed day equals the same search on that day's backup: the same results, ranks and mode.\n   280\t8b. **The index:**\n   281\t   - a build failing after one batch leaves only correct vectors, and the next build embeds the rest;\n   282\t   - a store replaced at the same path, with the same first step, whose view `v1` has a different text: the old `v1` vector is not\n   283\t     used, and search is lexical and lists `v1` as missing;\n   284\t   - with the same text, the vector is used, being correct by its key;\n   285\t   - a store restored from an earlier snapshot: only its visible views' vectors are used;\n   286\t   - a later day completing while the service runs: a new view with no matching vector is listed missing and search is lexical until a\n   287\t     restart, after which it is embedded and search is semantic; a new view whose matching vector is already in the shared index is\n   288\t     used at once;\n   289\t   - a service started on a first-day store, through the API's and MCP's own start-up: after the first day completes, search is\n   290\t     lexical when a visible view lacks a matching vector, and semantic when the shared index already holds one for every visible\n   291\t     view; after a restart it is semantic;\n   292\t   - an invisible view (an unfinished day's) whose cached vector has a different length does not change a search at the last\n   293\t     completed day: the same results, mode and `missing_vectors`;\n   294\t   - two concurrent searches for one new query: both answers are well formed, and the cache holds one vector for it; no equality with\n   295\t     a fresh embedding is asserted;\n   296\t   - an index build committing between a search's vector read and its scoring changes nothing in that search's answer.\n   297\t   - All through the API's and MCP's own start-up and search entry points.\n   298\t8d. **Orderly stop:** with a service reader holding the store, the writer's stop leaves WAL mode in place, records it and releases the\n   299\t   lock; preflight then accepts the store. With no reader, the store ends in rollback-journal mode.\n   300\t8c. **The index:** on a store with an unfinished day, the views sent to the (fake) embedding service are exactly those visible as of\n   301\t   the last completed day.\n   302\t8d′. **Production matching unchanged:** `store.search_views` gives the same results as before on a stored ledger, and the matching\n   303\t   tests pass unchanged.\n   304\t9. **The resolver keeps legacy and empty stores as today:** a legacy ledger with no `days_completed` and an empty store give the\n   305\t   same answers before and after `resolve_bound` is introduced.\n   306\t10. **The paused-writer harness** (`page-contract/serving_while_writing.py`) re-run: every check holds and the first-day case passes.\n   307\t\n   308\t## Row 3's product-facing contract (council rounds 120 to 123; A1, clamping, withdrawn)\n   309\t\n   310\t**The rule.**\n   311\t\n   312\t- **On every store with a `days_completed` table**, finished or not, partial rows or not, an explicit upper bound later than the last\n   313\t  completed day is refused. The same date gets the same answer whenever it is asked.\n   314\t- An omitted bound gives the last completed day, as today.\n   315\t- **Steps but no completed day:** refused as the first day.\n   316\t- **`days_completed` present with no steps and no completed day** (an empty new store): read as today. An omitted bound gives what\n   317\t  `default_as_of` gives today (none), and an explicit bound passes.\n   318\t- **A ledger without the `days_completed` table:** today's default already queries that table (`store.last_completed_day`), so such\n   319\t  a ledger already fails today with SQLite's \"no such table\" for an omitted bound. Through the resolver, an explicit bound fails\n   320\t  the same way too, where before it skipped the default and could answer: a change, with none among the 143 stored ledgers.\n   321\t- **Ranges** (amended in council round 124): a start after its resolved end is refused, for `changes` and `history` alike. A start\n   322\t  equal to the end reads as before: an empty window for `changes`, whose start is excluded, so a polling client keeps its empty\n   323\t  answer until a day completes; and that one day for `history`, whose start is included.\n   324\t\n   325\t**The owner, and what the structural check enforces.**\n   326\t\n   327\t- **`service.resolve_bound(conn, bound)`** raises `FirstDayNotCompleted` or `BoundNotCompleted(requested, last_completed_day)`, and is\n   328\t  idempotent: a bound it has already passed passes again. On an empty store (tables, no steps, no completed day) or a legacy ledger,\n   329\t  an omitted bound resolves to what `default_as_of` gives today, which can be `None`.\n   330\t- **`service.resolve_range(conn, start, end)`** resolves `end` through `resolve_bound`, then raises `RangeNotOrdered(start, end)`\n   331\t  when the start is after the end (round 124). When the end resolves to `None` (an empty store), it compares nothing and keeps\n   332\t  today's outcome.\n   333\t- `error_response` maps all three exceptions.\n   334\t- **Every public function that takes a date resolves it at its entry through `resolve_bound`:**\n   335\t  - `service`: `summaries`, `views`, `view`, `evidence`, `history`, `record`, `changes`, `evaluate`;\n   336\t  - `bundle`: `not_judged`, `view_bundle`, `days`, `thesis_bundles`, `library`;\n   337\t  - `idea.read_idea` and `search.search`.\n   338\t- Nested calls pass a resolved bound, which resolves again unchanged. That covers `view_bundle` called directly with no date (as\n   339\t  `scripts/mock_data.py` does), or from `_bundles` and `idea.read_idea`.\n   340\t- **Internal helpers that take a date** (`dependencies`, `dependants`, `evidence_record`, `is_open_as_of`, `state_as_of`, `visible`,\n   341\t  `_bundles`, …) receive their caller's resolved bound. That is `None` on an empty store, as today, and they behave as today with it.\n   342\t  Each public function's output on an empty store is the same before and after the change; the test records today's outputs first.\n   343\t- **A view not visible at the resolved day is refused** (`KeyError`, 404) by `bundle.view_bundle` and `bundle.not_judged` when called\n   344\t  directly, as `_bundles` already skips it. So a question and its choices created after that day are never returned.\n   345\t- **The structural check** reads every function of `service`, `bundle`, `search` and `idea` with a date parameter (`as_of`,\n   346\t  `to_day`). Each must either call `resolve_bound` (or `resolve_range`) or be named in the check's list of internal helpers.\n   347\t  - A function in neither, or a bound resolved by `or default_as_of(...)`, fails the suite.\n   348\t  - The check's own mutant (a new public function without the call) must fail it.\n   349\t\n   350\t**The response, per channel.**\n   351\t\n   352\t| Channel | First day not completed | Bound after the last completed day | Range start not before its end |\n   353\t|---|---|---|---|\n   354\t| API | 503 `{\"error\": \"the store's first day has not completed\"}` | 409 `{\"error\": \"day not completed\", \"requested\": X, \"last_completed_day\": Y}` | 409 `{\"error\": \"range start after its end\", \"start\": S, \"end\": T}` |\n   355\t| MCP tools | a tool error whose message carries the same fields | the same | the same |\n   356\t| Streamed Idea route | the status and body, before any header | the same | — |\n   357\t\n   358\t- `error_response` returns `(status, payload)`. The three new exceptions get the payloads above, and every other error keeps\n   359\t  `{\"error\": message}`. `_answer` and `_stream_answer` send the payload as given.\n   360\t- **The streamed route, in one transaction:**\n   361\t  - it opens its connections, begins the read transaction and resolves the bound and first-day check;\n   362\t  - **only then** does it send its 200 headers and run the Idea work in the same transaction, which it ends after;\n   363\t  - a refusal is sent as a normal status and body before any header.\n   364\t  - The Idea work includes model calls, so this transaction lasts the whole request, up to minutes. That is within row 5's\n   365\t    one-request rule, and it holds back a checkpoint that long.\n   366\t\n   367\t**Callers.**\n   368\t\n   369\t- **The page, when its live library read succeeds:** it sends only days from the served dataset (`library.getDay()` to `/ideas`;\n   370\t  the returned `card.idea.as_of` to `/thesis-bundles`). These are never after the last completed day.\n   371\t- **The page, when its live library read fails:**\n   372\t  - it keeps its embedded snapshot silently (as `thesis-ui.md` specifies), and that snapshot's day can be later than the service's\n   373\t    last completed day;\n   374\t  - `/ideas` is then refused with 409, and the page shows its existing Idea error naming no day;\n   375\t  - this is documented here and tested; it is not claimed impossible.\n   376\t- **MCP clients and direct API users with a later date:**\n   377\t  - on the running product's store, they would get an error naming 2026-08-28 instead of today's half-processed 2026-08-29 (99\n   378\t    evidence records, 117 links);\n   379\t  - on a finished store, they would get an error instead of today's identical answer.\n   380\t  - This is the behaviour change, recorded for Alex.\n   381\t- **Not deployed:** no running service is restarted. D439 does not authorize a deploy, and the change reaches the running product\n   382\t  only on a restart, which is on Alex's page.\n   383\t\n   384\t**Proofs added to the plan (2b to 2e):**\n   385\t\n   386\t- 2b. The structural check, and its mutant.\n   387\t- 2c. Each public function called directly with a future bound, an omitted one and a past one, on the four store states (finished,\n   388\t  partial, first day, empty with tables) and a copy of the served store's state. Each call's actual output or error is compared\n   389\t  with the expected one: today's recorded output where the rule keeps it, the named refusal where the rule changes it.\n   390\t- 2c′. `view_bundle` and `not_judged` called directly for a view created after the resolved day are refused (404). `days()` on an\n   391\t  empty store returns `[]` as today.\n   392\t- 2d. The ranges: a start after its end is refused for both; equality reads as before (`changes` empty, `history` that day).\n   393\t- 2e. The streamed route on a first-day store and with a future date: a status before any header. With a writer committing during\n   394\t  an Idea request, the request's data are the state at its start.\n   395\t- 2f. The page verifier (`poc/ui/verify-*.mjs`): live read successful, then the Idea call succeeds; live read failed with a later\n   396\t  embedded day, then the Idea error shows and nothing else breaks.\n   397\t\n   398\t## The writer's start, stops and end (slice 4a; council rounds 131 and 132)\n   399\t\n   400\t**Opening in WAL mode before anything is written.**\n   401\t\n   402\t- `store.open_db` creates tables and commits as it opens, so WAL is set before it:\n   403\t  1. the writer creates the store file empty and exclusively (create-new);\n   404\t  2. it opens it with `sqlite3`, sets `journal_mode=wal` and **checks that the pragma answers `wal`**, refusing to start otherwise;\n   405\t  3. it closes;\n   406\t  4. it opens the store through `store.open_db`, which writes its schema in WAL mode (the mode is kept in the file);\n   407\t  5. **it sets `journal_size_limit` (64 MB) on that writer connection,** since the limit belongs to a connection, not to the file.\n   408\t- A test reads both `journal_mode` and `journal_size_limit` back on the writer's own connection after `open_db`, and again after the\n   409\t  first day.\n   410\t\n   411\t**Stops, through the real launcher (`run_stage_b.send`).**\n   412\t\n   413\t- **The stop:** `ladder_adapter.WriterStopped(reason, last_completed_day)`, an `Exception` that `run_stage_b.resendable` does not\n   414\t  accept.\n   415\t- **Where it is raised:** in `replay`, before a day when the free space is below 20 times the store's size (main file and `-wal`), or\n   416\t  when the truncating checkpoint after a completed day has failed for 3 consecutive days. Before raising, `replay` writes\n   417\t  `adapter-counts.json` with the stop's reason and the last completed day, so the partial result is on disk.\n   418\t- **What the launcher does with it:** its existing handling of a stop no resend can change, with one small change so the stop's\n   419\t  own reason reaches the run (round 132):\n   420\t  - `limit.stop(reason)`, where the reason for a `WriterStopped` is `\"<run> incomplete: <the stop's reason>\"` and not only the\n   421\t    exception's name;\n   422\t  - the attempt recorded with its reason;\n   423\t  - the draw's state `incomplete`;\n   424\t  - no redo, and every later launch stopped.\n   425\t  - It is never recorded as complete.\n   426\t- **The store keeps every completed day,** because the stop comes before a day starts or after a day's checkpoint. Readers keep\n   427\t  serving the last completed day.\n   428\t- **A stop before the first day** leaves the store empty (its tables, no step), which readers read as an empty store, and is recorded\n   429\t  `incomplete` with its reason.\n   430\t- **Tests through `run_stage_b.send` with the fake judge:**\n   431\t  - low space before the first day;\n   432\t  - low space before the third day (the last completed day is the second);\n   433\t  - three failed checkpoints;\n   434\t  - in each case: the exact reason in the attempt **and** in the run-level record (`stopped`), the draw's state, no redo, the\n   435\t    store's last completed day, and the counts file's reason.\n   436\t\n   437\t**Its end.** After the last day the writer tries `journal_mode=delete` with a short busy timeout. If service readers hold the store,\n   438\tit stays in WAL mode and \"stopped in order, WAL kept\" is recorded in `adapter-counts.json`. Tested with and without a reader.\n   439\t\n   440\t**Limit of 4a, stated:** until slice 4b the writer takes no lock. 4a's proofs run on isolated test stores and do not make a live run,\n   441\tor preflight beside one, safe. P1 stays unresolved.\n   442\t\n   443\t## Who writes, and copies: slice 4b (council rounds 134 and 135)\n   444\t\n   445\t**The lock's owner and life.**\n   446\t\n   447\t- `replay` owns both the writer's connection and its lock, in one `try`/`finally`. It acquires the lock in `open_wal_store` before\n   448\t  the first write.\n   449\t- **`open_wal_store` cleans up after itself:** if anything fails after it has taken the lock and before it returns (the WAL pragma,\n   450\t  `store.open_db`, the identity check), it closes whatever connection it opened and releases the lock before raising. So `replay`\n   451\t  only ever owns a fully opened store. Tested by making `store.open_db` fail after the lock is taken: afterwards the lock is free at\n   452\t  once and no connection is open.\n   453\t- **On every exit** (a normal end, `WriterStopped`, a judge or schema error, a spending stop, an interrupt, a failure while opening)\n   454\t  it first closes the writer's connection, then releases the lock. No writable connection outlives the lock.\n   455\t- No caller uses the runner's connection after `replay` returns (searched: `stage_b/*.py`, `serving_while_writing.py`).\n   456\t- Tested on each exit: afterwards the connection is closed, and a new `acquire` takes the lock at once.\n   457\t- **A killed writer process** (a subprocess running `replay`, ended with `SIGKILL` after committed partial-day writes) leaves the\n   458\t  lock released by the operating system:\n   459\t  - preflight then takes it and copies;\n   460\t  - the copy answers as the last completed day;\n   461\t  - this replaces 4a's in-process interrupt as the crash proof for the lock.\n   462\t\n   463\t**The snapshot helper** (`live_lock.snapshot(store_path, destination)`, used by `scripts/snapshot_store.py`, its user-facing entry\n   464\tpoint).\n   465\t\n   466\t- **The same path rules as `acquire`:** the source's `realpath`, and a source with more than one hard link refused. It takes no lock,\n   467\t  since it is AGENTS §9's route beside a running writer.\n   468\t- **The destination must be a new path:**\n   469\t  - it must not exist, and must not resolve to the source;\n   470\t  - the copy is made into a temporary file beside it, converted out of WAL mode (`journal_mode=delete`, checked) and integrity\n   471\t    checked;\n   472\t  - it is then published **without replacing**: a hard link from the temporary file to the destination, which fails if the\n   473\t    destination exists, then the temporary file is removed. A plain rename could replace a file that appeared during the copy\n   474\t    (round 135).\n   475\t  - on any failure (the backup, the conversion, the check) the temporary file is removed, nothing is left at the destination, and\n   476\t    the helper raises.\n   477\t  - An incomplete copy is never offered as a snapshot.\n   478\t- **Tested through `scripts/snapshot_store.py`:**\n   479\t  - a snapshot while the writer commits;\n   480\t  - a refused hard-linked source;\n   481\t  - an existing destination;\n   482\t  - a destination equal to the source;\n   483\t  - a failing conversion (made to fail by a test hook on the helper's conversion step), leaving nothing at the destination;\n   484\t  - a destination created just before publication, which stays untouched while the helper refuses and removes its temporary\n   485\t    copy.\n   486\t\n   487\t**The filesystem-full proof is separate from the suite.**\n   488\t\n   489\t- It is `live-service/filesystem_full_proof.py`, run explicitly. It never fills the workspace's filesystem.\n   490\t- It creates a unique small disk image and mount point under the session's temporary folder (`hdiutil create`, a few MB;\n   491\t  `hdiutil attach`), and always detaches and deletes them in a `finally`.\n   492\t- It runs the writer on a store there until the filesystem is full. It checks that the writer exits as a crash and that readers\n   493\t  serve the last completed day, and it reports whether the reason was recorded.\n   494\t- If the image cannot be created or mounted, it reports **unexercised**, never passed.\n   495\t\n   496\t**Found while building 4b (D598).**\n   497\t\n   498\t- **A read-only reader could not open a WAL-mode store whose side files did not exist** (\"unable to open database file\"; tested on\n   499\t  this machine's SQLite 3.51.0 through Python 3.10, on scratch stores). A read-only connection cannot create the `-shm` it needs.\n   500\t  The product's own readers open `mode=ro` (`store.open_read_only`). The state can arise when the last connection to close is a\n   501\t  read-write one and the mode is still WAL. In two direct checks the writer itself did not leave it: the side files remained after\n   502\t  a stop. So what follows is a guard on that reachable state, not a fix of an observed writer failure.\n   503\t  - So the writer now leaves WAL mode on **every** exit (`LadderAdapter.close`), not only at its normal end: before closing its\n   504\t    connection it tries `journal_mode=delete`. WAL stays in two cases, kept distinct:\n   505\t    - **a reader holds the store:** that reader is read-only, so it leaves the side files in place when it closes;\n   506\t    - **the mode change fails for another reason** (for example the store's path changed): it is recorded as `wal`, and the\n   507\t      connection is still closed and the lock released.\n   508\t  - Tested on each exit: the store's mode is `delete` afterwards.\n   509\t- **Preflight read each store twice:** once for its checks (now under the lock) and once more, unlocked, for its keyed-views count.\n   510\t  It now makes one locked copy of each store before any part runs (`preflight.store_copies`), and both parts read that copy.\n   511\t  - Tested through `preflight.main` with the parts recording what they were given.\n   512\t- **`verify` reports a missing store and a newly added hard link** as a changed path (`PathChanged`), so the writer stops at its\n   513\t  next day instead of failing with another error.\n   514\t- **The filesystem-full proof** (`filesystem_full_proof.py`, run separately; `filesystem-full-proof.output.txt`), each variant on its\n   515\t  own new 2 MB disk image, both detached and deleted:\n   516\t  - **A, the free-space stop switched off:** the disk filled inside the tenth day. The writer exited with \"database or disk is\n   517\t    full\", and no reason was recorded beside the store (as expected for a crash). Nine completed days were kept; readers and\n   518\t    preflight's copy both serve 2026-07-09; the lock was free once the process had ended.\n   519\t  - **B, the stop as built:** before the first day the writer stopped with its reason recorded, because it needed 17.6 MB free\n   520\t    (20 times the 0.88 MB store, schema and `-wal` included) and the disk had 1.9 MB.\n   521\t\n   522\t**Round 137's corrections (D599).**\n   523\t\n   524\t- **Every reader opens a store by its own path.** `store.open_read_only`, which the API, MCP, `service` and reports all use,\n   525\t  resolves a symlink and refuses a store with another hard link (`live_lock.LockRefused`, naming the reason).\n   526\t  - The API refuses at start (`index_at_start`) and per request (500, the reason in the body).\n   527\t  - MCP refuses at start and per tool call.\n   528\t  - Census before the change: none of the 702 `.sqlite` files under `poc/data` and `clean-arch` has a second hard link.\n   529\t- **Cleanup when cleanup itself fails:**\n   530\t  - **`LadderAdapter.close`:** if leaving WAL mode fails, the connection is still closed and then the lock released. If closing\n   531\t    the connection fails, the lock is kept until the process ends (the operating system releases it then). So a writable\n   532\t    connection never outlives its lock.\n   533\t  - **`snapshot`:** if the target cannot be opened, the source is closed and nothing is left.\n   534\t  - **`scripts/snapshot_store.py`** also reports a SQLite error as a refusal (exit 1) instead of a traceback.\n   535\t- **A store moved mid-run, a finding, not understood and not fixed:**\n   536\t  - What was seen: SQLite raised \"disk I/O error\" on the writer's next statement after its store file was renamed, before the\n   537\t    writer's own path check. This happened only in a process that had first run the API/MCP hard-link test. Which SQLite state in\n   538\t    that process makes the difference was not identified.\n   539\t  - A moved store breaks the contract's operating precondition (a store's path is never replaced while it may be in use). This\n   540\t    finding is therefore a stated limit of that test case, not a claim that the writer handles a moved store.\n   541\t  - What the writer records no longer depends on it:\n   542\t    - its stop takes the last completed day from its loop;\n   543\t    - before each day it checks the path first, then any checkpoint failure.\n   544\t\n   545\t**Round 138's correction (D600): a reader holding the store is reported apart from every other failure.**\n   546\t\n   547\t- `truncate_checkpoint` answers `done`, `busy` or the error's own text. `busy` means SQLite's own \"database is locked\" messages,\n   548\t  or a checkpoint held back by another connection. That shows contention, not which connection; round 139 renamed the recorded\n   549\t  reasons to \"another connection held the store\".\n   550\t  - Only `busy` counts toward the three-day stop \"another connection held the store\".\n   551\t  - Any other error stops the writer before the next day with \"the truncating checkpoint after <day> failed: <error>\". After the\n   552\t    last day it is recorded in the counts.\n   553\t- `leave_wal` answers the mode it ends in and why WAL was kept: \"another connection held the store at the end\", or \"leaving WAL\n   554\t  failed: <error>\".\n   555\t- Tested with a connection whose checkpoint, or whose change out of WAL, fails with \"disk I/O error\":\n   556\t  - the stop's reason, and the record at the end, name that error and not a reader;\n   557\t  - the lock is free afterwards.\n   558\t- The real held-reader test still reports a reader.\n   559\t\n   560\t## Proof 10's design (council rounds 140 and 141)\n   561\t\n   562\t**What it runs.**\n   563\t\n   564\t- A new harness, `live-service/proof10_serving_while_writing.py`. It reuses the D573 harness's page rendering and checks, and leaves\n   565\t  that file as the record of its own run.\n   566\t- It re-runs the extension draw's c1 through the accepted loop into a new scratch store to 2027-04-02.\n   567\t  - Every judge request is answered from the stored archive by identity: $0, no model or embedding call.\n   568\t  - The writer is the built `replay`: WAL, its lock, the checks before each day, its end.\n   569\t- It runs detached with a log after the council's yes.\n   570\t\n   571\t**Starting the API.** The API is started on the store being written at the first pause, lexical. It is ready when `/days` answers\n   572\t200, or 503 with exactly \"the store's first day has not completed\". Any other answer fails the proof, and the D573 harness's \"known\n   573\tfinding\" exception is gone.\n   574\t\n   575\t**At each of the seven pauses (states labelled):**\n   576\t\n   577\t1. **The API, `/days` and `/library`:** every D573 check holds, the first-day one included.\n   578\t   - At 2026-09-22 before close (the first day, nothing completed), both answer 503 with that body.\n   579\t   - Fresh pages are rendered at the same two pauses as before.\n   580\t2. **MCP, a module-level check:** the MCP module is loaded once at the first pause, as its entry point builds it, lexical, and its\n   581\t   tool functions are called in-process. This exercises the tools' implementation, not the MCP transport, which stays a stated\n   582\t   limit.\n   583\t   - On the first day, its instructions say no day has completed, and `thesis_library()` and `views()` refuse.\n   584\t   - Afterwards, `thesis_library()`'s day and view ids equal `/library`'s.\n   585\t3. **Lock and copies:**\n   586\t   - `live_lock.acquire` is refused, and so is preflight's `store_copies`, which names the snapshot route;\n   587\t   - the store is in WAL mode.\n   588\t4. **Snapshots in two distinct states:**\n   589\t   - **2026-09-25 before judging, a day's articles committed and the day not completed:** the copy's last day is 2026-09-24 and it\n   590\t     holds processing steps dated 2026-09-25;\n   591\t   - **2027-01-31 before close, a due-day closing not yet completed (no articles that day):** the copy's last day is 2027-01-30 and\n   592\t     none of the three questions due that day (v3, v4, v7) is closed in it. A switch has no day of its own, so the first run's query\n   593\t     of a day column failed: my error, the harness's, not the product's.\n   594\t   - Both copies are out of WAL mode, with integrity `ok`.\n   595\t\n   596\t**Concurrent reads.** A poller asks `GET /library` back to back from the API's start to the writer's return. The harness brackets\n   597\teach day's completion with the times just before and just after `close_and_complete`. For every answer:\n   598\t\n   599\t- the status is 200, or 503 only for a request sent before the first day's completion ended;\n   600\t- the day it serves completed no later than the answer arrived (the day's completion started before the answer was received);\n   601\t- it is no older than the last day whose completion had ended before the request was sent;\n   602\t- the days never go backwards;\n   603\t- after the run, `GET /library?as_of=<that day>` decodes to the same JSON (compared by a hash of its canonical form). The first\n   604\t  difference is read before anything is called a leak; this is a test of the historical filter, not a guarantee row 3 already\n   605\t  gives.\n   606\t- **The polling must not be vacuous:** at least 20 answers, and at least one day whose completion has answers on both sides (one\n   607\t  served an earlier day and arrived before the completion started; one served that day or later).\n   608\t\n   609\t**After the writer ends:**\n   610\t\n   611\t- the lock is free;\n   612\t- the store is out of WAL mode, or WAL is kept with \"another connection held the store at the end\" recorded (the poller may hold a\n   613\t  read at that moment);\n   614\t- the API answers as of the store's last completed day.\n   615\t\n   616\t**It exits 1 unless every check holds.** A failure is read to its first differing value before anything is changed.\n   617\t\n   618\t## Slice 5: row 1's reader rules and the proofs still open (council rounds 143 and 144)\n   619\t\n   620\t**Row 1, made true in code** (a product change: today readers set no busy timeout of their own, and a failed open names no path):\n   621\t\n   622\t- **One opener for a reader's pinned state:** `service.open_pinned(path)` opens with `mode=ro`, runs `BEGIN` and the first read, and\n   623\t  returns the connection. The API's `Connections` and MCP's `connections()` both call it, replacing their own copies of those three\n   624\t  steps.\n   625\t- **Loud, with the store's path:** if the open, the `BEGIN` or the first read fails with a SQLite error, `open_pinned` closes what it\n   626\t  opened and raises `service.StoreUnreadable(\"<path>: <SQLite's message>\")`, a `LedgerError`.\n   627\t  - The API answers 500 with that message, before any header on the streamed route.\n   628\t  - MCP raises it from the tool.\n   629\t  - It never serves an empty or stale answer in its place.\n   630\t- **An explicit busy timeout:** `store.open_read_only` connects with `timeout=READ_BUSY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (5 s, the value Python\n   631\t  applied by default until now). SQLite's busy handler retries `SQLITE_BUSY` within it; there is no application-level retry, and\n   632\t  row 1's wording is narrowed to say so.\n   633\t\n   634\t**Tests, at $0:**\n   635\t\n   636\t- **6a.** A WAL store whose side files are absent, in a read-only directory:\n   637\t  - `open_pinned` raises `StoreUnreadable` naming the path;\n   638\t  - `GET /days` answers 500 with the path;\n   639\t  - the streamed Idea route answers 500 before any header;\n   640\t  - an MCP tool raises it.\n   641\t- **6b.** A WAL store held by a connection in exclusive locking mode, a stand-in for SQLite's rare WAL busy cases (such as WAL-index\n   642\t  recovery), which cannot be timed in a test:\n   643\t  - released after 0.5 s, a reader's `open_pinned` waits and succeeds;\n   644\t  - with the timeout set to 0.3 s and the hold kept for 1.5 s, it raises `StoreUnreadable` with \"database is locked\" and the path.\n   645\t- **5a.** `max_page_count` set on the writer's connection during a day, so the day fails with SQLite's \"database or disk is full\":\n   646\t  - through `run_stage_b.send`, the draw is incomplete with no redo;\n   647\t  - readers serve the last completed day;\n   648\t  - the lock is free.\n   649\t  - Kept beside the real-disk proof, which it does not replace.\n   650\t- **5b.** A real reader held across one completed day, then released:\n   651\t  - the checkpoint after that day is `busy`;\n   652\t  - the next one is `done`, and the `-wal` is then 0 bytes.\n   653\t- **7′.** In the `SIGKILL` test, the killed writer takes a reference backup through its own connection right after its second day\n   654\t  completes, before any write of the third. After the kill, the crashed store and preflight's copy are each compared with the\n   655\t  reference.\n   656\t  - The calls are `tests/bound_cases.calls`: 8 service functions, 5 bundle functions and lexical search.\n   657\t  - Each runs at the omitted bound, an explicit past bound (the first completed day), an explicit bound equal to the last completed\n   658\t    day, and a future bound (refused alike).\n   659\t  - Equality is of the outcome (the canonical JSON's hash or the error).\n   660\t  - The API and MCP routes call these functions. Idea functions are not in the list, and the report says so.\n   661\t- **8′.** On the same crashed store, `scripts/snapshot_store.py` through a symlink and through the own path gives two copies whose\n   662\t  every table is equal row by row.\n   663\t\n   664\t**Slice 5's results (D603).**\n   665\t\n   666\t- **Built:**\n   667\t  - `store.READ_BUSY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` and the explicit timeout;\n   668\t  - `service.open_pinned` and `StoreUnreadable`, used by the API's `Connections` and MCP's `connections()`.\n   669\t- **Tests:**\n   670\t  - `tests/test_reader_rules.py` (5): 6a through `open_pinned`, the API's `/days` and streamed Idea route (500 with the path,\n   671\t    before headers), and MCP at start and per tool; 6b waits about 0.5 s and then reads, and held past a 0.3 s timeout it fails\n   672\t    with the path. The exclusive-lock fixture was first checked to make the read wait.\n   673\t  - `stage_b/test_writer_proofs.py` (2): 5a, a `max_page_count` failure on the third day, incomplete with no redo, the lock free,\n   674\t    and readers on the second day; 5b, `busy` while a reader is held, then `done` with the `-wal` at 0 bytes.\n   675\t  - The `SIGKILL` test now does 7′ and 8′. 7′ is 88 outcomes over the listed 14 functions at 4 bounds in 2 states, the crashed\n   676\t    store and preflight's copy each equal to the reference taken before the third day. 8′ is the symlink and own-path snapshots\n   677\t    equal in every table.\n   678\t- The suite passes (346), the new system's folders (192), and the frozen checks. Mutants 6 of 6 (`slice5-mutants.txt`).\n   679\t\n   680\t## Not covered\n   681\t\n   682\t- Resuming a stopped live store.\n   683\t- The Idea planner on a new-system ledger.\n   684\t- A long-running service's restart and monitoring.\n   685\t- The page's note when its live read fails, which is Alex's presentation choice.\n     1\t> **Stale in part (2026-11-17, D653):** Alex has since chosen astra (D648) and its gate-1 run on the 44 was sent and read (D649); \"ready for your word\" below is historical. The current state and next step are in `reflection-8/LOG.md`.\n     2\t\n     3\t# What waits on Alex: one page for one sitting (2026-11-16, D575)\n     4\t\n     5\t**In one line:** the new system is not ready to replace the running one.\n     6\t\n     7\t- The step that writes each question's answer choices still misses your quality gate.\n     8\t- The test of its final answers has not passed either.\n     9\t- Making it fit into the product has moved a long way.\n    10\t\n    11\tSix choices are yours, and nothing below decides them for you. Until you choose, the running system stays the default (D544).\n    12\t\n    13\tThe two systems:\n    14\t\n    15\t- **The running system** is the pipeline that serves the product today.\n    16\t- **The new system** is the rebuilt pipeline (clean Steps 1 to 4). It reads news, opens questions, writes their answer choices and\n    17\t  judges them.\n    18\t\n    19\t## Your choices, in the order they were put to you\n    20\t\n    21\t### 1. Mark the pilot cards (Deck 1), about half an hour\n    22\t\n    23\t**What it is:** 19 cards from one stored week, with output from both systems; no card says which system wrote it.\n    24\t\n    25\t- 12 real answers and 2 planted wrong answers.\n    26\t- 2 matters no question took up.\n    27\t- 3 sheets of facts that never reached an answer.\n    28\t- 3 hidden repeats of earlier cards.\n    29\t\n    30\t**What you mark:**\n    31\t\n    32\t- On each answer card: is the question worth tracking (yes / no / cannot tell)? Is the answer supported by what is shown (yes / no /\n    33\t  cannot tell)?\n    34\t- On the two cards for matters no question took up: only whether the matter is worth tracking (there is no answer to mark).\n    35\t- On each fact sheet, for every fact, two marks: does it bear on the question (yes / no / cannot tell)? Would it change the answer,\n    36\t  only its confidence, neither, or cannot tell? The sheets include controls.\n    37\t- The deck's last box gives your marks as text to paste back; the counting needs the key held outside the repository.\n    38\t\n    39\t**Why it comes first:** since 2026-11-10 every judgement of quality has come from model readers, and their two families disagree. The\n    40\tdeck tests whether your own reading can check them. It ranks neither system and passes no gate.\n    41\t\n    42\t**Where:** [`pilot-deck-1/deck-1.html`](pilot-deck-1/deck-1.html), opened in a browser.\n    43\t\n    44\t### 2. What role should your six lines play for the answer-choice step?\n    45\t\n    46\t**Your six lines (D400):**\n    47\t\n    48\t1. No set of answer choices asks a different question from its topic.\n    49\t2. None leaves a part of its topic unlisted.\n    50\t3. None has an outcome with no matching answer, or with two.\n    51\t4. No refusal without a justified reason.\n    52\t5. At least 35 of the 44 topics right in at least two of three attempts, at least 28 of them with written answer choices.\n    53\t6. The same favourable direction in all three attempts for every company figure, and the same due day for at least nine in ten topics.\n    54\t\n    55\t(The later readings had fewer topics than 44; the council scaled line 5 to them as a diagnostic, not as your line.)\n    56\t\n    57\t**The record:** the gate has been missed in all nine readings. Not every line failed each time: in the latest reading line 4 was met,\n    58\tand line 6 could not be measured there (too few topics of the kinds it covers).\n    59\t\n    60\t**New (D568, D569):** we read the same 39 answer choices again with fresh readers of the same two model families.\n    61\t\n    62\t- Every line came out the same as before.\n    63\t- The same seven attempts failed line 2.\n    64\t\n    65\tThat is repeatability under one method. It is not proof that each mark is right.\n    66\t\n    67\t**Also new:**\n    68\t\n    69\t- **Line 2's field had no reader.** The parts a question leaves unjudged were shown nowhere until today; they now appear beside each\n    70\t  answer.\n    71\t- **One reading mark checks a field still unread.** It is not one of your six lines but a mark the readers give (an event's stated plan,\n    72\t  part of the reference); that field is read nowhere in the product.\n    73\t- **A paper recount:** with both of those marks set aside (the unjudged-parts mark and the event-reference mark), 9 or 7 of 13 topics\n    74\t  come out right, against 11 needed. The recount was not done with only the reference mark set aside.\n    75\t\n    76\t**Options:**\n    77\t\n    78\t- Keep all six lines, read with every mark, as the gate.\n    79\t- Decide separately what role each disputed mark should play (the unjudged-parts mark, now shown on the page; the event-reference\n    80\t  mark, read nowhere).\n    81\t- Set new lines after your Deck 1 reading.\n    82\t\n    83\t**Tried since (D577 to D580):** the step was told plainly that the topic's wording means both the description and the outcome.\n    84\t\n    85\t- In a small test it listed more of the omitted parts.\n    86\t- It also declined twice where both readers said it should not.\n    87\t\n    88\tWe have no reviewed repair. The records do not give a general rule for which part is judged when a topic's outcome names several\n    89\tthings (for example, whether a product's volume shipments or its first revenue is the thing tracked). Some of the strict counts\n    90\talso got worse (`unlisted-parts/RESULT.md`).\n    91\t\n    92\t**Since then (D582 to D588; you were away, so the council decided):**\n    93\t- **Your rule added:** a second side of the same bet stays in the question as context (D436). With it (arm A2), the step left no\n    94\t  part unlisted on the test topics, and it stopped declining wrongly.\n    95\t- **What went wrong instead:** it now also lists things the answer already decides. For example, it lists \"the deal is terminated\"\n    96\t  beside a question whose answer choices include exactly that.\n    97\t- **One more fix tried (A3):** it removed some of those entries. It added a neighbouring kind. The model readers disagree among\n    98\t  themselves on every one of these kinds, and none of them is one of your six lines. So we stopped adjusting this edge.\n    99\t- **The test on unread news (D589, D590), the week of 10-13:** the system opened only 5 new questions, too few independent ones\n   100\t  for a verdict, so the comparison closed without one. On those 5 questions:\n   101\t  - the current step still leaves a part unlisted on one question (your line 2);\n   102\t  - on another, it lets \"Ruled out\" catch a mere postponement (your line 3);\n   103\t  - its refusals were all justified (your line 4).\n   104\t  - A2 did no worse anywhere, and A2 is still not accepted.\n   105\t- **The two model reader families disagree on the due day of company figures in annual reports.** Your rule (D414) sets that day\n   106\t  in code. Read alone, both families now apply your rule, so the reader instructions were left unchanged.\n   107\t- **A new question for you (D591): when a company announces that something will happen later than a question's date, is the\n   108\t  question \"ruled out\" or \"still open\"?**\n   109\t  - **The example:** \"Will Vera Rubin start shipping by 31 December 2026?\" Nvidia announces that first shipments move to 2027.\n   110\t  - **The current step writes this as \"ruled out\"** on 37 stored answers, and the model readers mostly accept it.\n   111\t  - **For a deal's closing or a government's rules moved past the date, the readers lean to \"still open\".** No rule of yours\n   112\t    covers the difference.\n   113\t  - **Option 1, \"ruled out\" (recommended, not your existing rule):** a deadline bet counts as lost when the one who decides\n   114\t    announces a slip past the date. Cost: on the card, a delay and a cancellation look the same.\n   115\t  - **Option 2, \"still open\":** the card shows no decision, and the question closes without the event having happened. Cost: about\n   116\t    37 stored answers become failures of your line 3, and the step needs a repair.\n   117\t  - **Until you choose:** nothing changes, and the 38 affected answers are listed beside every gate count\n   118\t    (`unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/postponement/BOUNDARY-CASES.md`).\n   119\t- **The final-answer test (D592):** it can compare only 4 questions with your reference where 8 are needed. No single repair the\n   120\t  council can make reaches 8: delivering three missed facts reaches 7, and the eighth needs changes to two steps at once. A new\n   121\t  reference set would be your choice.\n   122\t- **Running the new system live (D593; your choice 6):** the rules a live database must follow were written and agreed after nine\n   123\t  rounds of review: how readers see one consistent day, what happens on a crash or a full disk, and who may copy it. Next they are\n   124\t  proven at no cost on test databases. Nothing runs live, and the one exception to the working rule \"never open a live run's\n   125\t  databases\" is only proposed, for you.\n   126\t\n   127\t**Later the same day (D623 to D632; the council decided):**\n   128\t- **The readers are not too harsh** (D625). A careful writer wrote answers for 26 topics outside your 44, and the readers found no\n   129\t  fault in any of them.\n   130\t- **On those topics, the current step breaks one of your lines 1 to 4 in about 1 attempt in 6.**\n   131\t- **More thinking time did not help** (D626).\n   132\t- **A stronger model from the same provider** (\"sol\"; its published price is 20 times the current model's, and per answer it cost about 9 times more) broke a line in 5 attempts of 78, against\n   133\t  the current step's 13 (D630).\n   134\t  - It declines more often: 24 times against 15.\n   135\t  - Most of its extra declines are on topics whose outcome names several things.\n   136\t  - The readers disagree on those declines.\n   137\t- **A new question for you (D631): when a topic's outcome names several things, which one is the question about?**\n   138\t  - An independent check found that no rule of yours decides it. Five stored topics show it, each with two readings:\n   139\t    - **Optical-switch shipments:** the full year 2027 is the question, or the whole span from late 2026 through 2027 is.\n   140\t    - **Chip sales into China:** U.S. approval is the question and China's acceptance a condition, or the two governments' decisions\n   141\t      are two separate things.\n   142\t    - **An acquisition:** signing is the question and closing follows, or signing and closing are two separate things.\n   143\t    - **Investment commitments:** the remaining balance is the question and the cash paid is context, or each is its own figure.\n   144\t    - **Memory prices:** the contract-price change is the question and the makers' margins are context, or these compete. Separately:\n   145\t      is \"HBM/DRAM\" one usable price, or must one series be chosen?\n   146\t    - **A chip export licence (added later):** lifting the U.S. licence requirement is the question and resumed China revenue its\n   147\t      consequence, or the two are separate things.\n   148\t  - **Until you choose:** nothing changes, and the stronger model stays a candidate, not adopted\n   149\t    (`reflection-6/model/rule-audit.md`).\n   150\t- **The strongest model tried (D632 to D634):** \"astra\", whose published price is 100 times the current model's. It ran against\n   151\t  \"sol\" on all 26 topics in two halves, three attempts each, $3.72 in all. These are measurements of today's unchanged instructions.\n   152\t  - **Against your lines 1 to 4:** astra failed 4 attempts of 78 and sol failed 9. Every one of astra's 4 is a decline on a topic whose\n   153\t    outcome names several things.\n   154\t  - **Checked against the articles by two reviewers (D636):** 11 of astra's 51 written questions would make the judge record the wrong\n   155\t    thing, and your six lines do not count them:\n   156\t    - 4 date a signed deal's completion at the next annual report instead of the topic's date;\n   157\t    - 3 date an event at its period end while the report that proves it comes later;\n   158\t    - 3 widen \"this quarter\" to the year-end;\n   159\t    - 1 invents a way of averaging two prices.\n   160\t- **A one-sentence fix for the first of those (D636, D637):**\n   161\t  - **The cause:** our instructions told the step that an obligation \"already taken on\" is checked at the next annual report. Your\n   162\t    D210 meant that for amounts, such as how much is owed. The stronger models applied it to \"will the deal close\", against your D403\n   163\t    rule for events: the topic's date or its period end.\n   164\t  - **The fix:** narrowing the sentence to amounts. In a small test on one deal written two ways, the date followed the topic 4 times\n   165\t    out of 4, against 0 of 4 before, and nothing else moved.\n   166\t  - **The verdict was \"no conclusion\",** because the two reviewers split on one unrelated question.\n   167\t  - **Its status:** kept beside the frozen instructions as a proposed fix, not adopted. You can undo it with a word.\n   168\t- **Ready for your word: the gate-1 measurement on your 44 topics (D643).** It is prepared and not sent.\n   169\t  - **Pick one of two configurations:** astra on today's instructions, or astra with the two sentences below.\n   170\t  - **Cost and spending limit:** about $5 to $7 to run, and set the limit at about $9.\n   171\t  - **How to launch:** write one small authorization file, then run one command.\n   172\t  - **What is known and what is not:** in `reflection-6/gate1-candidate/README.md`.\n   173\t- **One more candidate tried (D639 to D641):** astra with your D436 sentence (\"another side of the same bet stays as context, and is\n   174\t  not by itself a reason to decline\").\n   175\t  - It wrote the export-licence question (H20) both times, where the unchanged instructions declined both times. No reviewer found\n   176\t    a fault in those questions.\n   177\t  - On memory prices it also wrote questions, but the reviewers disagree.\n   178\t  - Both readings were voided by their own checks: one reader family failed planted test cases on whether a listed item is \"already\n   179\t    decided by the answer choices\", so that side effect is unmeasured.\n   180\t  - It stays an untested-enough candidate, not adopted.\n   181\t- **For you, with both readings:**\n   182\t  - **An event dated at its period end whose proof arrives later** (for example, \"first Vera Rubin revenue in fiscal 2027\", proven by\n   183\t    the 10-K in March). Should the judge decide on the day, or wait for the report? Your D403 sets the day. When to read the proof\n   184\t    is the judging step's choice.\n   185\t  - **The readers' instructions** still state the wide \"annual report\" rule for every obligation. In practice both reader families\n   186\t    already preferred the topic's date.\n   187\t\n   188\t**Until you choose:** the gate stays as written and stays unmet.\n   189\t\n   190\t### 3. What should the next product test start from? (D559)\n   191\t\n   192\t**The choice:**\n   193\t\n   194\t- **Option A:** confirm that the next product-oriented test starts from a real library of questions already held, built by an earlier\n   195\t  run. Tests that start from empty can stay as diagnostics.\n   196\t- **Option B:** choose another starting condition.\n   197\t\n   198\t**Evidence, with its limits (D561 to D564):**\n   199\t\n   200\t- **Small comparison, 11 articles:** read once from a held library and once from empty. The held library opened 4 new questions where\n   201\t  the empty start opened 16. It did not open a separate equity-commitments question that the empty start opened; those facts went under\n   202\t  a held question, and both reader families accepted that home. By the rule written before the run, the result fell between its two\n   203\t  outcomes.\n   204\t- **The fortnight run, one draw:** week 2's six new questions repeated none of the held ones. This cannot show the problem is solved in\n   205\t  general.\n   206\t\n   207\t**Until you choose:** each test states its own starting condition.\n   208\t\n   209\t### 4. Answers placed with nothing behind them (D520)\n   210\t\n   211\t**The problem has two cases, which need not get the same answer:**\n   212\t\n   213\t- **Case one:** nothing shown to the judge sizes the amount, yet it names a band.\n   214\t- **Case two:** the estimate rests on an unsupported step the judge supplied itself (a discount, a growth rate), and that step decides\n   215\t  the band. (A step the judge supplies is not wrong in itself; the case is the unsupported one.)\n   216\t\n   217\t**Options for each case:**\n   218\t\n   219\t- Show the band as today.\n   220\t- Show it with a plain label of what it rests on.\n   221\t- Show no band.\n   222\t\n   223\tNo recommendation is recorded among these.\n   224\t\n   225\t**What was tried (D565, D566):**\n   226\t\n   227\t- **Asking the judge:** asked after the fact which band its own evidence sized, the judge did not separate the answers readers had\n   228\t  failed from those they had passed.\n   229\t- **Two reader families:** they mostly agreed on the narrower mark that no amount was the answer's starting point, and split on\n   230\t  whether a scaled comparable is a basis. That narrower observation came after the fact, and the check's own verdict was \"unclear\".\n   231\t\n   232\tNeither a label nor a \"no band\" rule has been built or tested.\n   233\t\n   234\t**Until you choose:** the band is shown, as today.\n   235\t\n   236\t### 5. Two directions the running product invents (D571); two separate choices\n   237\t\n   238\t**5a. Yes/No questions:** \"Yes\" is coloured as good news and \"No\" as bad. A \"Yes\" can be bad for the company. Change it, or keep it.\n   239\t\n   240\t**5b. Theme ranges:** the highest range is scored +2 by inference. For a theme such as memory prices, higher may be bad. Change it,\n   241\tor keep it.\n   242\t\n   243\t**What the new system does instead:**\n   244\t\n   245\t- Its events, and its theme ranges with no registered favourable direction, are shown in neutral colours, with no score.\n   246\t- An Idea's combined score is not computed when one of these is among its drivers, and the Idea card says which one.\n   247\t\n   248\t### 6. Running the new system live\n   249\t\n   250\t**Where it stands now (D594 to D603):** the design was resumed under D439, agreed, built and tested on test copies at no cost. Nothing\n   251\tis running live and nothing has been restarted.\n   252\t\n   253\t**What the tests show:**\n   254\t\n   255\t- While the new system writes a day, the website and the agent tools only ever show whole finished days.\n   256\t- On a brand-new database's first day, they say \"not ready yet\" instead of showing a half-done day (open defect 10 is fixed).\n   257\t- Only one writer can work on a database at a time.\n   258\t- A copy for checking is taken safely, and a crash or a full disk loses nothing that was already finished.\n   259\t\n   260\t**Example:** the test re-ran six months of one draw while asking the website for the library about 170 times. Every answer was a\n   261\twhole finished day, never part of one.\n   262\t\n   263\t**Three choices are yours:**\n   264\t\n   265\t1. **How live data is read.** Today's working rule never opens a running database directly, only a copy (AGENTS §9). The product's\n   266\t   own readers would need an exception (P1). This is proposed, not decided.\n   267\t2. **Restarting the running service.** It picks up the new behaviour only on a restart. Example: a date after the last finished day\n   268\t   would then be refused instead of answered from a half-done day.\n   269\t3. **When to adopt.** Adopting the new system at all still comes after the quality gates.\n   270\t\n   271\t**One small gap remains your choice:** if the page cannot reach the service, it quietly keeps the copy built into the page, as\n   272\tspecified. A \"collected snapshot\" note is a possible change.\n   273\t\n   274\t## What the council decided in the meantime, reversibly (D606, D607; each reversed on your word)\n   275\t\n   276\t- **The next product test** starts from a library built from real earlier material. Tests from empty stay as separate diagnostics.\n   277\t- **Your six lines** stay the gate, with their accounting unchanged.\n   278\t- **A company's formal decision that a dated event will not happen by its date** rules the event out. Example: \"NVIDIA will not ship\n   279\t  Vera Rubin in 2026\" answers \"will it ship by 2026-12-31?\" as ruled out. An authority's proceeding that goes on is still waiting.\n   280\t  This is the readers' majority view and my earlier lean; your ruling replaces it.\n   281\t- **The two product directions (D520, D571)** stay as the running product has them.\n   282\t- **Reading a live database directly (P1)** is not taken. The copy rule stays.\n   283\t\n   284\t## What is in force meanwhile\n   285\t\n   286\t- **The product:** the running system serves it, unchanged.\n   287\t- **The council:** works under D439, deciding reversibly and recording each decision. Any paid send needs its own decision, within the\n   288\t  $40 ceiling; about $26.18 is used.\n   289\t- **The new system's integration, checked on stored replays and serving tests:**\n   290\t  - the page shows its questions, including the parts each question does not judge, with no invented direction;\n   291\t  - due questions close on their due day, as in the running system;\n   292\t  - a freshly opened page shows the latest completed day.\n   293\t  - Still open: the first-day defect, the live-service design and the Idea planner on a new-system database.\n   294\t- **The final-answer test (D576):** it could compare only four questions with the reference, because the other questions, or the\n   295\t  facts shown to the judge, did not meet its control conditions; eight were required. This says nothing either way about the judge on\n   296\t  the excluded questions. Re-running the judge cannot change it.\n   297\t- **Next, at no cost (D576):** a count of the answer choices that leave a part of the question unlisted (your line 2), now that the\n   298\t  page shows that list; any change is brought to the council before it is tried.\n   299\t\n   300\t## Where the evidence is\n   301\t\n   302\t- **The record of this stretch:** decisions D559 to D575 (`docs/DECISIONS-log.md`).\n   303\t- **The round log:** §§53 to 57 (`LEAN-LOG.md`).\n   304\t- **The page and serving checks:** `page-contract/` (`RESULT.md`, `SERVE-RESULT.md`, `SERVING-WHILE-WRITING-RESULT.md`).\n   305\t- **The repeated reading:** `gate1-stick/RESULT.md`.\n   306\t- **The fortnight run:** `carried-run/RESULT.md`.\n   307\t- **The earlier page:** `DECISION-PAGE-2026-11-15.md`.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:1:# Run log: the release comparison (D665, D666)\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:11:| 2026-11-17 | plan | Round 2: Codex NOT READY, Claude reviewer NOT READY (7 changes); retry, binary-search and cost-curve findings checked against code and stored runs; lessons in ROUND-LOG.md; structural rule adopted (prose review stops at design level; build-level checked at the stage-0 freeze review) | — |\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:26:| 2026-11-17 | stage 0 | Size check (§1.7): v118's 14-day draws q1-q3 max judging request 114 KB / 29K input tokens; v34 end-state proxy 630 pieces (~500 KB, overstated: v34 linked wide) — watched, never a stop. Preflight (production, frozen v108 d1): A 2/2 schema refusals as stored, B 12/726 dropped (7 quote, 5 merged), C 0/170, D 0/43 — unchanged baseline | stage0/preflight-output.txt |\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:28:| 20:55 | slice | Launched s-new (clean, astra-frozen, ledger $10) and s-old (poc-v118, ledger $3), 07-01..07-07, on the shared items DB at 13bc220d | — |\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:30:| 22:05 | slice | s-old complete ($0.22). s-new stopped at the ladder: the shared ledger refused a $1.33 astra reservation against the slice's $10 guard (spent $2.86; 8 concurrent worst-case reservations ≈ $10.7). Diagnosis: guard sizing, a launcher parameter (plan §2: fix the launcher only, rerun). Before the stop: Step 1 98 articles ≈ 40 s and $0.011 each; Step 2 and cases complete (9 cases); 5 astra ladder sends answered, no wrapper refusal. Full-run guard $65 leaves room (≈ $23 spent + ≈ $11 reservations at that point) | rerun as slice-2, 07-01..07-03, guards $25 / $3 |\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:32:| 2026-11-17 22:36 | run | **Full run launched:** new-1..3 (clean, astra-frozen, guard \\$65 each) and old-1..3 (poc-v118, guard \\$25 each), 07-01..09-20, on the shared items DB, all six within one minute (D667 timing), at commit 4c66408f | monitor armed (stops, crashes, component changes, progress every 100 attempts / 7 days) |\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:34:| 23:03 | check | six draws running; spend new 0.43–0.45, old 0.91–1.08; my own mistake: a status check tried to count rows in a live Step 1 trace (AGENTS §9: never open a live run's databases); refused by the classifier, not repeated; status is read from run.json and the ledgers only | run/ |\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:46:  - Any derived file that differs from the rebuild is refused.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:55:  - Step 2: 1473 articles, 5256 records, 2327 groups. Outcomes: 2181 new, 2929 repeat, 146 revision; none refused. Later linking judged\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:69:  - **Projection:** the first attempt cost $22.03. The redo should cost about $21, then Step 2 about $5, the ladder about $7, and the\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:83:- 2026-11-18 21:03: new-3 Step 2 and cases done; ladder started ($27.76). Step 2: groups 2324; outcomes {'new': 2193, 'repeat': 2956, 'revision': 131}; refused 0; several same_statement answers 1306; later linking: judged 28281, linked 135, pending 0. Cases: new-3: 60 cases written to poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/new-3/cases/cases.json (the trace opened 60)\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:102:    that one stored request, which is a cent and a few seconds (AGENTS §7.3, \"a model call is a sampler, re-send before calling it a\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-LOG.md:154:  - **Size of the class.** 7 of the 32 matters are company figures (M06, M07, M09, M10, M11, M18, M30). The old system serves 5–6 of\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:1:# The product comparison that decides release (D665): run plan, version 12 (D668–D672 amendments)\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:20:## 0. Scope\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:23:- poc-v118 against the new system (astra-frozen), from empty at 2026-07-01, three draws each;\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:37:## 1. Stage 0 ($0): built, proved, frozen, then reviewed\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:39:### 1.1 Versions manifest\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:42:- every file either system runs, including poc-v118's configuration and the frozen ladder request;\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:46:The launchers refuse a file that differs.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:48:### 1.2 Exposure census, exclusions, one input\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:102:### 1.3 Blind matter census (articles only; frozen before any send)\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:121:### 1.4 The verdict rule (`verdict-rule.json`, hashed; `score_comparison.py` with a test and a mutant per rule)\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:198:### 1.5 The new system as run: code changes C to F, each with a test and a mutant\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:201:  max_calls 12,000 (1,976 articles × 2 format tries × 3 network attempts, the true bound) and max_seconds 172,800. It is hashed.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:204:  - At the transport, for **every** request (first sends and retries with the refusal note), it builds the luna request from the same system\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:205:    text, user text and schema through the same code, and refuses any difference other than the model string.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:208:  - The test includes a refused-then-retried send.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:219:### 1.6 What D665 prints beside, with its tool and input\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:228:### 1.7 Long-run risk, checked at $0\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:230:- **The old judge's request size.** poc-v118's `evidence_in_view` (`views/judging.py:41`) shows every arrival since the fifth most recent\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:233:  - Code computes the size of every request v118's rule would build on it.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:237:### 1.8 Preflight\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:245:## 2. Stage 1: the slice\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:255:## 3. Stage 2: the full run\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:271:- **A stopped ledger** refuses later launches. A relaunch would be a diagnostic only (§1.4 item 10).\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:273:## 4. Stage 3: reading and verdict\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:288:   - the cost and the time.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:292:## 5. Estimates (measured rates; checked at the slice)\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:301:**Old system, per draw:** poc-v118's own $0.53 to $0.66 for 243 articles, scaled up, is about $5. But cost per article rises as the library\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:309:## 6. Monitoring\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:314:  - production's `replay.log` day and cost lines;\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:316:    completed judge days, the largest judge request and refused judge answers.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:318:  - every component state change, stop, crash, refusal or redo;\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:335:- **Watched and reported, never a stop:** the largest judge request and refused judge answers. A day the judge halts on is D449's to handle.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/RUN-PLAN-v12.md:338:## 7. Returns to Alex\n10308:## D689 · 2026-11-20 · Alex chooses option 1 (keep old, pause the hybrid's release line); council N closes the judge and c8 lines for now\n10360:## D690 · 2026-11-20 · Alex chooses A: poc-v118 is the product, the research line rests until his new direction; the D681–D689 records are committed locally\n10362:- **Alex's words** (2026-11-20), replying to the two items put to him after D689: \"1. OK 2. A\". Item 1 was a local commit of the\n10363:  D681–D689 records, not pushed. Item 2's options were:\n10370:  conditions of D689 stand. B and C are not taken.\n10371:- **Done:** the D681–D690 records are committed on `clean-arch`, and nothing is pushed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
